inal': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf84b834-8d23-558b-b1a1-686a41c876a4', 'word_count': 1337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf84b834-8d23-558b-b1a1-686a41c876a4'}, {'abstract': 'Bill de Blasio will need to make difficult changes in the next contract with teachers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/02/opinion/the-new-mayor-and-the-teachers.html', 'snippet': 'Bill de Blasio will need to make difficult changes in the next contract with teachers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor-elect Bill de Blasio will take office facing the need to forge new labor agreements with the unions that represent nearly all of New York City’s 300,000 municipal workers. The largest of these, the United Federation of Teachers, is in a particularly sour mood. Representing 40 percent of the city’s work force, the union has been without a contract since 2009. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New Mayor and the Teachers', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The New Mayor and the Teachers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Performance Evaluations (Labor)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seniority Systems', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Federation of Teachers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T02:13:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc34e573-b0d3-5a14-960c-13d6b90e055d', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc34e573-b0d3-5a14-960c-13d6b90e055d'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Duquette of the Baltimore Orioles, Neal Huntington of the Pittsburgh Pirates and Ben Cherington of the Boston Red Sox credit their former coach at Amherst College, Bill Thurston, for their success.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/02/sports/baseball/3-general-managers-true-to-one-school.html', 'snippet': 'Dan Duquette of the Baltimore Orioles, Neal Huntington of the Pittsburgh Pirates and Ben Cherington of the Boston Red Sox credit their former coach at Amherst College, Bill Thurston, for their success.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If becoming a baseball general manager is to be accepted into an old boys’ club, then the annual general managers meetings are their grand convention. The executives spend a few days each off-season sitting through presentations and talking baseball, ending each night with big dinners and drinks at the hotel bar until it closes. Then they do it all again, only bigger, at the winter meetings a few weeks later.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/02/sports/AMHERST4/AMHERST4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/02/sports/AMHERST4