 None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T12:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7153b97-b431-5e6e-a563-845bfaedbce9', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7153b97-b431-5e6e-a563-845bfaedbce9'}, {'abstract': 'A small-business owner tries to figure out precisely how many health insurance plans he has to choose from. Are we talking tens? Hundreds? Thousands? And in the process, he finds one he likes.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/02/how-i-found-a-surprisingly-good-health-plan/', 'snippet': 'A small-business owner tries to figure out precisely how many health insurance plans he has to choose from. Are we talking tens? Hundreds? Thousands? And in the process, he finds one he likes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve written four posts on health insurance recently, and there are more to come. I have been trying to get a sense of what I am buying, of how many options I have to consider, of how much it will cost and of how I can explain my choices to my workers. This has been extraordinarily complex.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How I Found a Surprisingly Good Health Plan', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Downs, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AETNA INC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blue Cross and Blue Shield Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HealthCare.gov', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paul Downs Cabinetmakers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Healthcare Insurance Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philadelphia (Pa)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T12:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d374873-93dd-5d8d-adbe-0f0d10a3eedc', 'word_count': 1861, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d374873-93dd-5d8d-adbe-0f0d10a3eedc'}, {'abstract': 'As thousands of protesters demanded his resignation, President Viktor F. Yanukovich seemed to brush aside the unrest and defended his refusal to sign agreements with the European Union.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/03/world/europe/ukraine-unrest.html', 'snippet': 'As thousands of protesters demanded his resignation, President Viktor F. Yanukovich seemed to brush aside the unrest and defended his refusal to sign agreements with the European Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KIEV, Ukraine — Even as thousands of protesters occupied Independence Square, blockaded the Cabinet Ministry and continued to demand his resignation, President Viktor F. Yanukovich of Ukraine on Monday defended his refusal to sign accords with the European Union, said he was on the verge of securing lower gas prices from Russia, and urged opposition politicians to wait for presidential elections in 2015 to challenge him.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/world/europe/03Ukraine1_cnd/03Ukraine1_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/03/world/europe/03Ukraine1_cnd/03Ukraine