he Exuberance of Paul Smith', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Menkes, Suzy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Paul', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Design Museum (London, England)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T14:42:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzy Menkes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Menkes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3737abb-33a8-5471-a8be-5ea8097ab997', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3737abb-33a8-5471-a8be-5ea8097ab997'}, {'abstract': 'The prominent Chinese film director acknowledged that he had two sons and a daughter with his current wife but denied rumors that he had seven children with four different women.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/02/zhang-yimou-offers-to-pay-fine-for-one-child-policy-violation/', 'snippet': 'The prominent Chinese film director acknowledged that he had two sons and a daughter with his current wife but denied rumors that he had seven children with four different women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After months of silence on the issue, acclaimed Chinese film director Zhang Yimou has admitted to having two sons and a daughter with his current wife, in contravention of China’s family planning rules.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zhang Yimou Offers to Pay Fine for One-Child Policy Violation', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zhang Yimou', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jiangsu Province (China)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T14:54:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Qin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Qin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14373a6f-5407-54f3-9ec8-2e012c5d8a7d', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14373a6f-5407-54f3-9ec8-2e012c5d8a7d'}, {'abstract': 'If only.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/02/doing-macro-first/', 'snippet': 'If only.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mike Konczal makes a very good point about how we teach economics. He suggests that we should go back to the way Samuelson did it in 1948 — macroeconomics first, then micro. This, he suggests, would give students a better perspective on reality, even though all the same material would eventually be covered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Doing Macro First', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T14:59:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf592d4d-43c5-5e9d-80df-f8b7d7496a30', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf592d4d-43c5-5e9d-80df-f8b7d7496a30'}, {'abstract': 'A number of new research reports and surveys are finding that teenagers and adults are continuing to read print books and often prefer them over digital e-books.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/02/the-print-book-here-to-stay-at-least-for-now/', 'snippet': 'A number of new research reports and surveys are finding that teenagers and adults are continuing to read print books and often prefer them over digital e-books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I didn’t want to start reading print books again, but I honestly had no choice. My dog, Pixel, forced me to.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/29/technology/bits-books/bits-books-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/29/technology/bits-books/bits-books-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/29/technology/bits-books/bits-books-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/29/technology/bits-books/bits-books-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/29/technology/bits-books/bits-books-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Allure of the Print Book', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name