s E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Net Neutrality', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T22:21:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4331bd8-ebb9-5d2a-a659-44041dab49d4', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4331bd8-ebb9-5d2a-a659-44041dab49d4'}, {'abstract': '“Beetles and Other Insects,” an expanded edition of Bernard Durin’s 1980 work, gathers 60 color plates of all his known watercolor portraits of insects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/03/science/the-french-painter-bernard-durins-close-look-at-insect-life.html', 'snippet': '“Beetles and Other Insects,” an expanded edition of Bernard Durin’s 1980 work, gathers 60 color plates of all his known watercolor portraits of insects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The visionary poet William Blake tumbled into the universe through his infinite grain of sand. For the French painter Bernard Durin, the portal was a beetle.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Bugs Too Lovely to Squish', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Big Bugs Too Lovely to Squish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Durin, Bernard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beetles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-02T22:24:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Jennings', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jennings', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7670b4c2-b2c3-52fd-8b85-969084d103f6', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7670b4c2-b2c3-52fd-8b85-969084d103f6'}, {'abstract': 'In “Eminent Hipsters,” Donald Fagen begins with tales about his early years in Steely Dan, but halfway through, his book becomes a cranky account of being on the road for a recent summer concert tour.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/03/books/eminent-hipsters-by-donald-fagen-of-steely-dan.html', 'snippet': 'In “Eminent Hipsters,” Donald Fagen begins with tales about his early years in Steely Dan, but halfway through, his book becomes a cranky account of being on the road for a recent summer concert tour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you like Steely Dan’s greatest hits too much, Donald Fagen of that band probably hates you already. You may be one of the “TV babies” who illegally download those songs, show up at his concerts expecting to hear familiar hits, have no patience for anything soulful or adventurous or obscure, yet rather humiliatingly are paying part of his rent these days. The phrase “TV babies” comes from the film “Drugstore Cowboy,” which used it to the same contemptuous effect.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1015, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/03/arts/BOOK/BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1015}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/arts/BOOK/BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 606, 'subType': 'jumbo', 