inal': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/58bf2b3d-dbbb-517d-b0e5-b4f61afbb981', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/58bf2b3d-dbbb-517d-b0e5-b4f61afbb981'}, {'abstract': 'Recreating the Greenwich Village of the 1960s, using the East Village, Brooklyn and Chinatown.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/magazine/positively-fourth-street-all-over-again.html', 'snippet': 'Recreating the Greenwich Village of the 1960s, using the East Village, Brooklyn and Chinatown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One primary pleasure of the Coen brothers’ new movie, “Inside Llewyn Davis” — a film that is abundant in its pleasures — is revisiting a New York that most New Yorkers thought was gone for good: Greenwich Village of the early 1960s, when downtown was teeming with bohemians and dreamers and not with lost tourists holding upside-down maps. “Davis” follows a Bob Dylanesque folk singer named Llewyn Davis and unfolds mostly (with a few detours to far-flung uptown and Chicago) in that fabled realm of a lost New York, complete with cold-water flats and smoke-filled subterranean clubs.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Positively Fourth Street, All Over Again', 'kicker': 'Shoptalk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Positively Fourth Street, All Over Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sternbergh, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Ethan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coen, Joel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Inside Llewyn Davis (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-03T21:03:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Sternbergh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sternbergh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7350b5a-4414-59cb-a308-b87de8fa01b9', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7350b5a-4414-59cb-a308-b87de8fa01b9'}, {'abstract': 'Despite modest progress by the Syrian government, Valerie Amos said, aid delivery remained far below what is needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/04/world/middleeast/top-un-relief-official-says-syria-crisis-worsening.html', 'snippet': 'Despite modest progress by the Syrian government, Valerie Amos said, aid delivery remained far below what is needed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNITED NATIONS — The top emergency relief official at the United Nations expressed new alarm on Tuesday at the humanitarian crisis caused by Syria’s civil war, telling the Security Council that despite some modest progress, both the government and insurgent sides were still impeding urgent deliveries of food and medicine to millions of desperate civilians.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syria Crisis Is Worsening, U.N. Relief Official Says ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syria Crisis Is Worsening, U.N. Relief Official Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amos, Valerie Ann', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-03T21:03:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02221232-cf13-5a39-bd0e-014b8e78e8dd', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02221232-cf13-5a39-bd0e-014b8e78e8dd'}, {'abstract': 'In this Text to Text, we pair Dr. King’s “I Have a Dream” speech with a reflection by the Times literary critic Michiko Kakutani, who explores why this speech has such lasting power.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/03/text-to-text-i-have-a-dream-and-the-lasting-power-of-dr-kings-dream-speech/', 'snippet': 'In this Text to Text, we pair Dr. King’s “I Have a Dream” speech with a reflection by the Times literary critic Michiko Kakutani, who explores why this speech has such lasting power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last summer was the 50th anniversary of the March on Washington, when the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. painted his dream of racial equality and justice for the nation that still resonates with us. “I have a dream,” he proclaimed, “my four little children will one day live in a nation where they will not be judged by the color of their skin but by the content of their character.” In this Text to Text, we pair Dr. King’s pivotal “I Have a Dream” speech with a reflection by the Times literary critic Michiko Kakutani, who explores why this singular speech has such lasting power.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/08/28/us/sub-jp-KINGSPEEC