ne}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed468fb8-c50d-5837-80e5-0b8b0f27dbf4', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed468fb8-c50d-5837-80e5-0b8b0f27dbf4'}, {'abstract': '“Out of the Furnace” looks at the visceral bond between two troubled brothers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/04/movies/out-of-the-furnace-with-christian-bale.html', 'snippet': '“Out of the Furnace” looks at the visceral bond between two troubled brothers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In movies, the working class often serves as a sacrificial emblem of the failure of the American dream, one that these days is often embellished with lovingly photographed decay and an elegiac air. Set in a corroded stretch of the Rust Belt, “Out of the Furnace” ups the ante with a story of two blue-collar brothers — a steel mill welder and a former soldier — who are as totemic as the figures immortalized in a Works Progress Administration mural. It’s a heavy, solemn tale of blood ties that turns into a melodramatic gusher filled with abstractions about masculinity, America and violence, but brought to specific, exciting life by Christian Bale, Casey Affleck and Woody Harrelson.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/arts/04OUT/04OUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/04/arts/04OUT/04OUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/arts/04OUT/04OUT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/arts/04OUT/04OUT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1301, 'width':