type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddb1acd2-c5ef-5288-81a4-fa10fcff3400', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddb1acd2-c5ef-5288-81a4-fa10fcff3400'}, {'abstract': 'Why are there still no automated safeguards for out-of-control trains like the one that jumped the tracks in the Bronx?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/04/opinion/lessons-unlearned-on-rail-safety.html', 'snippet': 'Why are there still no automated safeguards for out-of-control trains like the one that jumped the tracks in the Bronx?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a Metro-North train jumped the tracks in the Bronx on Sunday morning, killing four people and injuring more than 70, it was going 82 miles per hour, on a 30 m.p.h. curve. Why it hit that horrific speed — whether because the engineer was sleeping, distracted or incapacitated, or because of some catastrophic mechanical failure — is a question for the National Transportation Safety Board, the Metropolitan Transportation Authority, which runs Metro-North, and criminal investigators and prosecutors. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lessons Unlearned on Rail Safety', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lessons Unlearned on Rail Safety', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroad Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metro-North Commuter Railroad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metrolink (Southern California Regional Rail Authority)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Transportation Safety Board', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York Metropolitan Area', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T02:54:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/caa10eb3-a565-5d92-aace-e56cd9c5aa4f', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/caa10eb3-a565-5d92-aace-e56cd9c5aa4f'}, {'abstract': 'Quotation of the day for Wednesday, December 4, 2013.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/04/pageoneplus/quotation-of-the-day-for-wednesday-december-4-2013.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the day for Wednesday, December 4, 2013.', 'lead_paragraph': '“This once proud and prosperous city can’t pay its debts. It’s insolvent. It’s eligible for bankruptcy. But it also has an opportunity for a fresh start.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day for Wednesday, December 4, 2013', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T02:57:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/0894d977-53ef-506d-a536-37bb61fa9edb', 'word_count': 36, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0894d977-53ef-506d-a536-37bb61fa9edb'}, {'abstract': 'New research indicates that a marine reserve helps ward off some of the effects of climate change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/04/opinion/sustaining-resilience-at-sea.html', 'snippet': 'New research indicates that a marine reserve helps ward off some of the effects of climate change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the face of it, the value of a marine reserve — the equivalent of a national park or wildlife preserve on land — seems obvious. The oceans are in trouble, and setting aside regions of biodiversity, where fishing is strictly limited, if not prohibited, is one of the few effective means of protecting many species at once. But politically, there is nothing simple about creating marine reserves in international waters. Recently, China and Russia succeeded in blocking, yet again, the creation of a large marine reserve in Antarctica. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sustaining Resilience at Sea', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sustaining Resilience at Sea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiversity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maria Island National Park (Tasmania)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nature Climate Change (Journal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T02:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/22