', 'snippet': 'Felena Hanson noticed that most co-working spaces seemed to be dominated by men with tech companies. She opened Hera Hub, which she intends to franchise, to help women-owned businesses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was 1996, and 22-year-old Felena Hanson was driving through an intersection in San Diego to meet friends when a fire truck smashed into her car. She broke 27 bones in her arms, legs and face. “I was in a wheelchair for about three months,” she said. “I was told I would never run again. What a great wake-up call to really give you some perspective on life.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Car Accident, an Entrepreneur Decides to Think Big', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Franchises', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Diego (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T12:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colleen DeBaise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeBaise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63be6465-45c0-5257-928e-3aafdfec577a', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63be6465-45c0-5257-928e-3aafdfec577a'}, {'abstract': 'Though the government is largely ignoring protesters’ demands, it has said the police are barred from using force against peaceful demonstrators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/world/e