tname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47451b6f-a39f-5554-9837-15d2bfc589fe', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47451b6f-a39f-5554-9837-15d2bfc589fe'}, {'abstract': 'Some experts fear that the United States’ reliance on imported enriched uranium may affect its global nonproliferation efforts and potentially cause supply problems in the future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/us/politics/ebb-in-uranium-enrichment-in-us-raises-questions.html', 'snippet': 'Some experts fear that the United States’ reliance on imported enriched uranium may affect its global nonproliferation efforts and potentially cause supply problems in the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Energy Department officials will shake hands and exchange congratulations with Russian representatives at a Port of Baltimore dockside ceremony on Tuesday as a freighter from St. Petersburg is to arrive with the final shipment of enriched uranium that was once intended for Soviet warheads. The uranium will be turned into fuel for American power reactors, completing a 20-year program, called Megatons to Megawatts, that has eliminated enough highly enriched uranium to fuel about 19,000 warheads.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ebb in Uranium Enrichment in U.S. Raises Questions About Nuclear Policy ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ebb in Uranium Enrichment in U.S. Raises Questions About Nuclear Policy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Uranium', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'USEC (United States Enrichment Corp)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Depa