ight': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Today’s Scuttlebot: Snapchat Steals an Executive From Facebook, and the Future of Imgur', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T23:26:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13662018-2d40-5a19-8ee3-a3a70f06a1d4', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13662018-2d40-5a19-8ee3-a3a70f06a1d4'}, {'abstract': 'An online photography project created by a pair of Brooklyn artists explores the ways we make romantic connections online.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/04/dear-internet-thank-you-for-introducing-us/', 'snippet': 'An online photography project created by a pair of Brooklyn artists explores the ways we make romantic connections online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every couple has a “how we met” story, usually a sweet account of the moment their eyes met across a crowded room or how a chance encounter led to love. How many of those tales now include a mention of dating apps like Tinder, messages exchanged on a dating site, or a particularly thoughtful remark on an Instagram photo that led to coffee and beyond?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/technology/04bits-metonline-1/04bits-metonline-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/04/technology/04bits-metonline-1/04bits-metonline-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/technology/04bits-metonline-1/04bits-metonline-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/technology/04bits-metonline-1/04bits-metonline-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1100, 'width': 1650, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/technology/04bits-metonline-1/04bits-metonline-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/04/technology/04bits-metonline-1/04bits-metonline-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/04/technology/04bits-metonline-1/04bits-metonline-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dear Internet, Thank You for Introducing Us', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dear Internet: Thanks for Introducing Us', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Dating', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T23:27:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3549aa27-a394-5b88-819f-bc3369d0d022', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3549aa27-a394-5b88-819f-bc3369d0d022'}, {'abstract': 'An entire Jean Prouvé house was packed into crates and shipped to Design Miami for display.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/garden/in-miami-a-house-comes-to-visit.html', 'snippet': 'An entire Jean Prouvé house was packed into crates and shipped to Design Miami for display.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Patrick Seguin is known for his Paris gallery, his friendship with Jean Nouvel, who designed it, and his treasure trove of 17 restored houses by Jean Prouvé. He has exhibited a house here and there, in Paris and Turin, but he has never before had his workers pack a whole Prouvé house into 10 crates and ship it to America. The 8X8 house (8 meters by 8 meters, or about 26 feet by 26 feet) is one of two 1945 Prouvé houses Mr. Seguin found in Bezaumont, France. (The other is a historical monument.) Made in a factory and easily assembled and taken apart, they were an elegant, thrifty solution to the postwar housing crisis in France. The 8X8 house is on display at Design Miami in Miami Beach through Sunday, furnished with pieces by Prouvé and Pierre Jeanneret. In Miami, it would make a high-status pool house. “No need to be a billionaire to go for one of these houses,” he said, but the price is $2.5 million. Information: patrickseguin.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05ROOMS_SPAN/5ROOMS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 190, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05ROOMS_SPAN/5ROOMS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 190}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05ROOMS_SPAN/5ROOMS_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05ROOMS_SPAN/5ROOMS_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05ROOMS_SPAN/5ROOMS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05ROOMS_SPAN/5ROOMS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05ROOMS_SPAN/5ROOMS_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Miami, a House Comes to Visit', 'kicker': 'Currents | Rooms', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Miami, a House Comes to Visit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Linda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prouve, Jean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeanneret, Pierre', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami (Fla)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T23:28:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96151f02-8ba9-55df-b895-18b62d9bc501', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96151f02-8ba9-55df-b895-18b62d9bc501'}, {'abstract': 'Bisazza introduces new motifs by the Milanese designer Paola Navone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/garden/patterns-with-stories-to-tell.html', 'snippet': 'Bisazza introduces new motifs by the Milanese designer Paola Navone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bisazza, a leading Italian producer of tile, is introducing two glass mosaic patterns by the Milanese designer Paola Navone, whose globally inflected style is becoming familiar to American audiences, thanks to her collaborations with Anthropologie and Crate & Barrel. For one pattern, Affresco, she translated a traditional floral design into something colorful, grainy and surrealistic, aiming to make the images look as if plaster had been peeled away to unveil frescoes beneath the surface. Halo-Halo, her black-and-white take on Pop Art and graffiti, which she describes as “perhaps pictograms by some distant civilization or a board game from an imaginary place,” is available in nine motifs that can be used in a multitude of configurations. Bisazza also introduced its first ceramic tiles, designed by Antonio Citterio, collaborating with Patricia Viel, and Marcel Wanders. Affresco is $44 to $68 a square foot; Halo-Halo is $324 a square foot. Information: 212-334-7130 or bisazza.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05TILES_SPAN/05CURR2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05TILES_SPAN/05CURR2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05TILES_SPAN/05CURR2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05TILES_SPAN/05CURR2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1128, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05TILES_SPAN/05CURR2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05TILES_SPAN/05CURR2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05TILES_SPAN/05CURR2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Patterns With Stories to Tell', 'kicker': 'Currents | Tiles', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Patterns With Stories To Recount', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Navone, Paola', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brick and Tile', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bisazza SpA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-04T23:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54b2b305-ec03-5733-97fe-5a3c84d255c0', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54b2b305-ec03-5733-97fe-5a3c84d255c0'}, {'abstract': 'Madeline Weinrib remains true to the spirit, if not the letter, of the Russian artist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/garden/pretty-but-wheres-the-kandinsky.html', 'snippet': 'Madeline Weinrib remains true to the spirit, if not the letter, of the Russian artist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers will be forgiven for not immediately seeing the connection between Vasily Kandinsky’s paintings and Madeline Weinrib’s new napkins for the Neue Galerie, where an exhibit of the Russian artist’s work is on view. “The idea was to collaborate, not copy Kandinsky,” Ms. Weinrib said via email from India. “I decided to reimagine one of my favorite patterns, using colors from Kandinsky’s ‘Fugue’ painting. I thought the combination of colors would complement a variety of dishes, with a modern unexpected punch.” That pattern, called Daphne, is usually hand-woven in silk Ikat. Ms. Weinrib used hand-printed cotton instead, which made possible the intricate design, as well as machine washing. A set of four napkins, one each in blue, celery, iris and navy, is $100 at the Neue Galerie Design Shop. Information: 212-628-6200, extension 496, or shop.neuegalerie.org.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05GOODS/05CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 522, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05GOODS/05CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 522}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/garden/05GOODS/05CURR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 891, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption'