umbers ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-05T02:07:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dd106c9-40f3-500e-8617-9320c9aa9f54', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dd106c9-40f3-500e-8617-9320c9aa9f54'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Obama said in a speech on the economy and the widening gap in national income that he will devote the rest of his presidency to the crucial issue of social mobility.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/opinion/the-president-on-inequality.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Obama said in a speech on the economy and the widening gap in national income that he will devote the rest of his presidency to the crucial issue of social mobility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The issues that have obsessed Washington for the last few months — the government shutdown, the broken health care website, the unrelentingly bitter tone of a stalemated Congress — mean very little to most Americans. For a broad swath of the country, what matters hasn’t changed since the recession, and it is economic anxiety. Six in 10 workers in a Washington Post poll last week said they were worried about losing their jobs, the highest number in decades. Many of the millions who are unemployed have reached new depths of despair.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The President on Inequality', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The President on Inequality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-05T02:08:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/86664c12-f3a1-5b72-80d7-561b150d03d1', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86664c12-f3a1-5b72-80d7-561b150d03d1'}, {'abstract': 'The Mets’ message for 2014, after five consecutive losing seasons, is that their fans must keep waiting for a better team.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/sports/baseball/as-yankees-take-big-bites-the-mets-nibble.html', 'snippet': 'The Mets’ message for 2014, after five consecutive losing seasons, is that their fans must keep waiting for a better team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Mets are confident in their starting rotation. They have a left-hander and four right-handers, power arms for the front end and proven, solid options for the back. All of the starters are younger than 30.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/sports/Y-KEPNER/Y-KEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 495, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/05/sports/Y-KEPNER/Y-KEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 495}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/05/sports/Y-KEPNER/Y-KEPNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 844, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_na