me Probabilities: Vulnerable Chiefs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kansas City Chiefs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Redskins', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-05T05:29:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bd08da2-8f77-5175-8e6c-b282b6c036c9', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bd08da2-8f77-5175-8e6c-b282b6c036c9'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '1 P.M. (Starz) TWELVE MONKEYS (1995) A prisoner from the future (Bruce Willis) is forced by his captors to travel back in time to the 1990s to stop a virus from being released in this science fiction tale directed by Terry Gilliam. Madeleine Stowe is the psychiatrist who believes his seemingly crazy story, and a mental patient (Brad Pitt) is top among his list of suspects. Writing in The New York Times, Janet Maslin said the movie is “fierce and disturbing, with a plot that skillfully resists following any familiar course. The film’s hero fears that he’s half-crazy, and for two hours Mr. Gilliam artfully keeps his audience feeling the same way.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-05T06:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/716bbcbf-3ee6-5c7a-9d84-2a78b6895d62', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/716bbcbf-3ee6-5c7a-9d84-2a78b6895d62'}, {'abstract': 'When Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr. visits China, he does not let the serious matters at hand dampen his mood.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/covering-biden-in-beijing-and-becoming-the-story/', 'snippet': 'When Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr. visits China, he does not let the serious matters at hand dampen his mood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steve Clemons, a reporter for The Atlantic, was the “pool” reporter covering Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr. in Beijing on Thursday. That meant he got special access to the vice president, but also had to feed the rest of the press corps details and color on the visit. And he more than rose to the occasion, particularly when he became part of the story. The following are excerpts of his report from the Diaoyutai State Guesthouse. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Covering Biden in Beijing, and Becoming the Story', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-05T07:05:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gerry Mullany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mullany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'rep