: 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52e7d979-668b-5f85-9354-24bb7b4e75c7'}, {'abstract': 'Vanishing antiques shops, reappearing Frank Lloyd Wright stained-glass windows, and newly digitized in-house publications from the archives of decorative arts institutions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/06/arts/design/antiques-stores-fall-to-online-offerings.html', 'snippet': 'Vanishing antiques shops, reappearing Frank Lloyd Wright stained-glass windows, and newly digitized in-house publications from the archives of decorative arts institutions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Manhattan antiques shops founded decades ago are vanishing at an alarming rate, unable to compete with online offerings. No trace remains of Elliott Galleries’ presence on East 79th Street; its white marble storefront has been razed. Yale R. Burge Antiques, on East 62nd Street, is closing and will auction inventory at Doyle New York in January. The former home of Cove Landing, on Lexington Avenue, has sat empty while the proprietor, Angus Wilkie, tucks inventory into his apartment storage spaces and organizes short-term displays at the W. M. Brady gallery on East 80th Street.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/ANTIQUES5/ANTIQUES5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/ANTIQUES5/ANTIQUES5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 735}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/ANTIQUES5/ANTIQUES5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 836, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/ANTIQUES5/ANTIQUES5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height':