btype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/JPBOOKDICKINSON3/JPBOOKDICKINSON3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1638, 'width': 1364, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/JPBOOKDICKINSON3/JPBOOKDICKINSON3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/JPBOOKDICKINSON3/JPBOOKDICKINSON3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/JPBOOKDICKINSON3/JPBOOKDICKINSON3-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Poet Who Pushed (and Recycled) the Envelope', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Poet Who Pushed (and Recycled) the Envelope', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Werner, Marta L', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickinson, Emily', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bervin, Jen', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-05T21:53:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b2d56a3-3bac-5398-8b39-18b7f5b50d4d', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b2d56a3-3bac-5398-8b39-18b7f5b50d4d'}, {'abstract': 'This collection of Nora Ephron’s writings includes the novel “Heartburn,” scripts for “When Harry Met Sally . . . ” and “Lucky Guy,” and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/the-most-of-nora-ephron.html', 'snippet': 'This collection of Nora Ephron’s writings includes the novel “Heartburn,” scripts for “When Harry Met Sally . . . ” and “Lucky Guy,” and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a time when Nora Ephron was frequently compared to Dorothy Parker, even by Nora Ephron. (“All I wanted in this world was to come to New York and be Dorothy Parker. The funny lady. The only lady at the table. The woman who made her living by her wit.”) I believe we have all gotten over that. As Ephron herself figured out long ago, the idea of Dorothy Parker was far superior to the living version, who was good with a one-liner but not much fun to be around. The real Nora Ephron was the person every\xadbody wanted to hang out with, in part because she was funny and charming but more critically because she made the people she was with feel funny and charming. (I certainly wanted to hang around her, and we were friends for years.) She was never the life of the party in the classic center-of-the-crowd sense. She was the one who listened and then finally tossed in the one fabulous line that brought every\xadthing together. Her best writing was exactly the same.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-colli