ion_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'Zena Barakat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barakat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/5ef32248-ee0d-50b8-91cd-6b5eb695923c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/5ef32248-ee0d-50b8-91cd-6b5eb695923c'}, {'abstract': 'Memories of Nelson Mandela by Suzanne Daley, who was Johannesburg bureau chief from 1995 to 1999.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/05/world/africa/memories-of-mandela-statesman-dandy-flirt.html', 'snippet': 'Memories of Nelson Mandela by Suzanne Daley, who was Johannesburg bureau chief from 1995 to 1999.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As president, Nelson Mandela had so many requests for interviews that he frequently met with journalists in groups of three or four — often at 7 a.m. too, because he got up early. He never allotted much time for questions, sometimes just half an hour, and his temper flared easily, so watch out. In many ways, he was more a chief statesman than the head of a government. He didn’t like defending — or talking about — policy. And he had no patience for critics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Statesman, Dandy, Flirt', 'kicker': 'Memories of Mandela', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mandela, Nelson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-05T22:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzanne Daley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizati