None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T02:31:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc3dcae4-1195-5125-9b41-c889720ea12b', 'word_count': 16, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc3dcae4-1195-5125-9b41-c889720ea12b'}, {'abstract': 'Lamar Hill and two friends recorded some of the first hip-hop records as the Treacherous Three. Today, he is searching for full-time work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/06/nyregion/a-pioneer-of-rap-music-craves-9-to-5-stability.html', 'snippet': 'Lamar Hill and two friends recorded some of the first hip-hop records as the Treacherous Three. Today, he is searching for full-time work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lamar Hill began his first career on a central Harlem street corner, in the pre-MTV days of boomboxes and mix tapes. He and two pals, calling themselves Kool Moe Dee and Special K, recorded some of the earliest hip-hop records as the Treacherous Three. Wearing a T-shirt with an iron-on decal bearing his preferred name, L. A. Sunshine, he plugged his speakers into streetlight outlets to blast what was then a bold and unfamiliar new music, called rap.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/nyregion/neediest/neediest-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/06/nyregion/neediest/neediest-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/nyregion/neediest/neediest-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'supe