gacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-SHORTLIST/1208-bks-SHORTLIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-SHORTLIST/1208-bks-SHORTLIST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Views', 'kicker': 'The Shortlist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York Views', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Washburn, Alexandros E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cooper, Becky (Author)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T15:55:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54d7708b-b924-531d-8b51-2fa6b6e64514', 'word_count': 1043, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54d7708b-b924-531d-8b51-2fa6b6e64514'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE INVENTOR AND THE TYCOON: The Murderer Eadweard Muybridge, the Entrepreneur Leland Stanford, and the Birth of Moving Pictures,  by Edward Ball. (Anchor, $16.95.) At the height of his genius, Eadweard Muybridge, a British immigrant whose advancements in photography in the late 1800s gave rise to the motion picture industry, killed his wife’s lover in cold blood. Ball weaves an account of that crime, and the sensational trial that followed it, into the larger story of Muybridge’s complicated relationship with his patron, the railroad magnate Leland Stanford.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '82', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T15:56:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef669e0e-a309-54c0-af58-07e429417d78', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef669e0e-a309-54c0-af58-07e429417d78'}, {'abstract': 'Financial elder abuse statistics are not easy to pin down. It can also sometimes be hard to recognize that there is a problem.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/06/tracking-a-thief-once-you-know-there-is-one/', 'snippet': 'Financial elder abuse statistics are not easy to pin down. It can also sometimes be hard to recognize that there is a problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time my father was ever robbed, decades ago, he was vacationing in England with my mom. A certified public accountant, he had certain fiscal habits, one of which was to count and order his money, highest denomination bill to lowest, all facing the same way in his wallet. He did this constantly, usually at home after work. In England, though, he performed the rite in the hotel pub. My mother was furious. For good reason. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tracking a Thief, Once You Know There Is One', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Abuse', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bafcfb14-a948-5833-9893-470bb64c4c15', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bafcfb14-a948-5833-9893-470bb64c4c15'}, {'abstract': 'James Patterson’s “Cross My Heart,” new at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, is Patterson’s seventh novel to reach that list in 2013.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/15/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'James Patterson’s “Cross My Heart,” new at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, is Patterson’s seventh novel to reach that list in 2013.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No. 1 Dad: James Patterson’s latest thriller, “Cross My Heart,” is new at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list. It’s Patterson’s second Alex Cross book to top the list this year and, impressively, his seventh novel to reach the hardcover list in 2013. (That’s not counting appearances on the middle-grade list, the young adult list, the mass-market paperback list, the e-book list or the manga list — and the year isn’t over yet.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08list-img/08list-img-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:01:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1973e1b4-d08a-55d6-816e-0ccf120fcf04', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1973e1b4-d08a-55d6-816e-0ccf120fcf04'}, {'abstract': 'The yearly avalanche of cookbooks offers something for everyone, from “Fifty Shades of Kale” to “The Eat-a-Bug Cookbook.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/heston-blumenthals-historic-heston-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'The yearly avalanche of cookbooks offers something for everyone, from “Fifty Shades of Kale” to “The Eat-a-Bug Cookbook.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the seasonal cookbook offerings include titles like “Fifty Shades of Kale” and an updated edition of “The Eat-a-Bug Cookbook,” choice is not a problem. The yearly avalanche includes something for everyone. There are books from star chefs and famous restaurants, the latest go-rounds from tried-and-true food writers, the now customary exercises in vegetable-worship, armchair-tourist cookbooks and one book that defies description.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-Cooking/1208-bks-Cooking-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-Cooking/1208-bks-Cooking-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 520}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-Cooking/1208-bks-Cooking-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 888, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-Cooking/1208-bks-Cooking-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1647, 'width': 1899, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-Cooking/1208-bks-Cooking-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-Cooking/1208-bks-Cooking-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208-bks-Cooking/1208-bks-Cooking-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooking', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:02:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7fe0070-4095-5d51-b9c3-f46f9d03299d', 'word_count': 2100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7fe0070-4095-5d51-b9c3-f46f9d03299d'}, {'abstract': 'The Heath at the McKittrick Hotel in Chelsea is a recreated supper club complete with a jazz band and a faux smoky room where five finance types gathered recently.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/nyregion/a-speakeasy-experience-at-the-heath.html', 'snippet': 'The Heath at the McKittrick Hotel in Chelsea is a recreated supper club complete with a jazz band and a faux smoky room where five finance types gathered recently.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somewhere between the formality of a dinner theater and the cheesiness of a theme restaurant, the Heath at the McKittrick Hotel is — to use the phrase favored by marketing professionals — “a culinary experience.” Opened two weeks ago by Punchdrunk, the British theater company that also runs that immersive — sorry — theatrical experience, “Sleep No More,” in the same hotel in Chelsea, the Heath is a recreated speakeasy/supper club, complete with a jazz band, suspendered barmen, and haze of fake (i.e., health department-compliant) cigarette smoke. “When the band started playing,” said Ian Smith, one of five finance types who ate at the Heath last week, “I felt like I was in an episode of ‘Boardwalk Empire.’\xa0”\xa0', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/nyregion/08SUBTABLE_SPAN/08SUBTABLE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/nyregion/08SUBTABLE_SPAN/08SUBTABLE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/nyregion/08SUBTABLE_SPAN/08SUBTABLE_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/nyregion/08SUBTABLE_SPAN/08SUBTABLE_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/nyregion/08SUBTABLE_SPAN/08SUBTABLE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/nyregion/08SUBTABLE_SPAN/08SUBTABLE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '