ank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a3df5d0-f30c-5b3e-bc1e-f548e84e20f8', 'word_count': 795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a3df5d0-f30c-5b3e-bc1e-f548e84e20f8'}, {'abstract': 'David Thomson’s latest book is full of assured declarations, chatty asides and free-associative essays accompanying images from 71 films.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/david-thomsons-moments-that-made-the-movies.html', 'snippet': 'David Thomson’s latest book is full of assured declarations, chatty asides and free-associative essays accompanying images from 71 films.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The moment held forever on the cover of “Moments That Made the Movies” is a lulu: Joe Gillis floating facedown in Norma Desmond’s swimming pool in “Sunset Blvd.” The water is lit from below; the palette is 5 a.m. City of Angels black-and-white. Gazing at the image even in silence, anyone who loves movies can conjure the voice-over narration of the dead man, talking to the audience directly, describing to us how he, “just a movie writer with a couple of B pictures to his name,” has wound up at the decaying mansion of a faded movie star with two shots in his back and one in his stomach. The poor dope. Maybe an advanced movie lover can even hear the unique voice of the speaker — William Holden as Gillis — telling the tale that won the picture 11 Oscar nominations in 1951. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208SCHWARZBAUM/1208SCHWARZBAUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208SCHWARZBAUM/1208SCHWARZBAUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':