n lyrics to an embryonic version of Bruce Springsteen’s “Born to Run” were sold for $197,000 at an auction at Sotheby’s on Thursday, a spokesman for the auction house said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27artsbeat-springsteen/27artsbeat-sprinsteen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27artsbeat-springsteen/27artsbeat-sprinsteen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 772}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27artsbeat-springsteen/27artsbeat-sprinsteen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27artsbeat-springsteen/27artsbeat-sprinsteen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1593, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27artsbeat-springsteen/27artsbeat-sprinsteen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27artsbeat-springsteen/27artsbeat-sprinsteen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27artsbeat-springsteen/27artsbeat-sprinsteen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Springsteen’s Handwritten Lyrics to ‘Born to Run’ Sell for $197,000', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Manuscripts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springsteen, Bruce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:13:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/523a8739-4ba4-5e7b-a8ed-5da1bbfe2760', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/523a8739-4ba4-5e7b-a8ed-5da1bbfe2760'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Gordon profiles the singers, songwriters and producers behind Stax Records.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/robert-gordons-respect-yourself.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Gordon profiles the singers, songwriters and producers behind Stax Records.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Knock on Wood,” “Soul Man,” “Hold On, I’m Coming,” “In the Midnight Hour,” “These Arms of Mine,” “I’ve Been Loving You Too Long,” “Green Onions,” “I’ll Take You There,” “Who’s Making Love” and, of course, the theme from “Shaft”: The list of hits produced by Stax could fill this page by itself. But Robert Gordon’s “Respect Yourself: Stax Records and the Soul Explosion” — “Respect Yourself” as performed by the Staple Singers, that is — is much more than a nostalgia trip or a soundtrack on paper. Gordon, a Memphis resident who has written books about his city’s music and a well-received biography of Muddy Waters, profiles singers, songwriters and producers. But he has also written a social history, viewing the company that for more than 15 years produced some of the most popular and important music in America as part of the history of Memphis and of the civil rights movement. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/arts/1208DIXLER/1208DIXLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/arts/1208DIXLER/1208DIXLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/arts/1208DIXLER/1208DIXLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/arts/1208DIXLER/1208DIXLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/arts/1208DIXLER/1208DIXLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/arts/1208DIXLER/1208DIXLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/arts/1208DIXLER/1208DIXLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sweet Soul Music', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sweet Soul Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stax Records', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elsa Dixler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elsa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dixler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc351895-fcfe-5267-b1c8-ba7a31b2ab0a', 'word_count': 1474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc351895-fcfe-5267-b1c8-ba7a31b2ab0a'}, {'abstract': 'Anjelica Huston’s memoir about growing up in Ireland, London and New York doubles as a social history of the 1960s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/a-story-lately-told-by-anjelica-huston.html', 'snippet': 'Anjelica Huston’s memoir about growing up in Ireland, London and New York doubles as a social history of the 1960s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before most American celebrity-watchers became aware of her as Jack Nicholson’s notably elegant girlfriend, in the mid-1970s — and before her acting career took off in 1985, when she was 34 (an Oscar award for “Prizzi’s Honor” and nominations for “Enemies: A Love Story” and “The Grifters”) — Anjelica Huston had a magical, if sometimes star-crossed, life in Ireland, England and New York. She was the daughter of the larger-than-life director John Huston (“The Treasure of Sierra Madre,” “The Maltese Falcon,” “The African Queen”), a man of great appetites and macho endeavor with whom “not only women but men of all ages fell in love . . . with that strange loyalty and forbearance men reserve for each other,” his daughter writes. Huston’s mother, Ricki Soma, a ballerina and the daughter of an Italian-restaurant owner in New York, was John Huston’s fourth wife. They eloped when she was 18 and he was in his mid-40s. Huston was their second child and — with her mother now transformed into the beautiful couture-clad “translucent and remote” mistress of a vast West Ireland estate — she was raised amid multiple servants, visiting film-world eminences and colorful locals in the kind of world that has disappeared, other than, say, in movies starring Keira Knightley. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '61', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208WELLER/1208WELLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 732, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208WELLER/1208WELLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 732}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208WELLER/1208WELLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 840, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208WELLER/1208WELLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1680, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208WELLER/1208WELLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208WELLER/1208WELLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208WELLER/1208WELLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Prequel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prequel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huston, Anjelica', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:15:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Weller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/681448be-44d3-58e4-bde6-c2b0675d5c25', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/681448be-44d3-58e4-bde6-c2b0675d5c25'}, {'abstract': 'Nicholas Dawidoff’s case study of the N.F.L., after a year spent with the New York Jets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/nicholas-dawidoffs-collision-low-crossers.html', 'snippet': 'Nicholas Dawidoff’s case study of the N.F.L., after a year spent with the New York Jets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Football, George Will once argued, “combines the two worst things about America: violence punctuated by committee meetings.” But in fact those “committee meetings,” with their militaristic strategies and tribal rituals, help explain the National Football League’s popularity in America. Sure, the violence might be problematic, especially in light of recent head trauma studies. But did you see that hit in the 49ers game?! Pass the bean dip.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '60', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208LEIBOVICH/1208LEIBOVICH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208LEIBOVICH/1208LEIBOVICH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 592}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208LEIBOVICH/1208LEIBOVICH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1011, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208LEIBOVICH/1208LEIBOVICH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1872, 'width': 1896, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208LEIBOVICH/1208LEIBOVICH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208LEIBOVICH/1208LEIBOVICH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208LEIBOVICH/1208LEIBOVICH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hard Knocks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hard Knocks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leibovich, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dawidoff, Nicholas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Rex', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:15:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Leibovich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leibovich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d52d6698-1f30-5da3-b8de-0afacdd04650', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d52d6698-1f30-5da3-b8de-0afacdd04650'}, {'abstract': 'John Shaw’s book tells the story of two American anthems: Irving Berlin’s “God Bless America” and Woody Guthrie’s “This Land Is Your Land.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/this-land-that-i-love-by-john-shaw.html', 'snippet': 'John Shaw’s book tells the story of two American anthems: Irving Berlin’s “God Bless America” and Woody Guthrie’s “This Land Is Your Land.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In late 1938, with the world on the brink of war, Kate Smith asked Irving Berlin, the world’s most famous songwriter, for a patriotic song she could sing on her annual Armistice Day radio broadcast. He dusted off and reworked a number he had begun writing in 1918 but abandoned. Before introducing the song to her vast national audience, Smith predicted that it “will be timeless — it will never die — others will thrill to its beauty long after we are gone.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208KEEPNEWS02/1208KEEPNEWS02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208KEEPNEWS02/1208KEEPNEWS02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208KEEPNEWS02/1208KEEPNEWS02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 473, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208KEEPNEWS02/1208KEEPNEWS02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 946, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208KEEPNEWS02/1208KEEPNEWS02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208KEEPNEWS02/1208KEEPNEWS02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208KEEPNEWS02/1208KEEPNEWS02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'American Songbook', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Songbook', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keepnews, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guthrie, Woody', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shaw, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berlin, Irving', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:16:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Keepnews', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keepnews', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/26e99582-cd85-5273-bc34-8af53e2c6578', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26e99582-cd85-5273-bc34-8af53e2c6578'}, {'abstract': 'Henry Bushkin, who worked as a lawyer for Johnny Carson, has written a gossipy, breezy memoir about the late-night host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/johnny-carson-by-henry-bushkin.html', 'snippet': 'Henry Bushkin, who worked as a lawyer for Johnny Carson, has written a gossipy, breezy memoir about the late-night host.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The day after he was hired by Johnny Carson, an obscure young lawyer named Henry Bushkin tagged along with him and some thugs in the night as they broke into an apartment searching for proof of Carson’s wife’s infidelity. They found plenty, as Bushkin tells it, and Carson leaned against a wall and wept, revealing the .38 he was carrying in case things went wrong. That was in 1970, when Carson had already settled into his role as superstar host of “The Tonight Show.” Over the next 18 years, Bushkin was his lawyer, fixer, friend and liege, “like all those guys on ‘Entourage,’ except there was only me.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208JAMES/1208JAMES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208JAMES/1208JAMES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 736}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208JAMES/1208JAMES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 835, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur