eb_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/06/cooking-light-with-seafood/', 'snippet': 'Light fish and seafood dinners make good choices for meals in between holiday parties, notes Martha Rose Shulman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many of you, still full from Thanksgiving, may be anticipating holiday parties to come and wondering how to balance things out with some of the meals in between. With this in mind, I decided to work on light fish and seafood dinners for this week’s Recipes for Health. They should provide you with a respite from rich food during this shortened window between Thanksgiving and Christmas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooking Light With Seafood', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sauces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seafood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:16:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a69a3bbd-2e70-53cd-9d77-c726ca58c736', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a69a3bbd-2e70-53cd-9d77-c726ca58c736'}, {'abstract': 'Virginia Postrel lays out the case for glamour as a life-shaping force, whether for good or for ill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/virginia-postrels-power-of-glamour.html', 'snippet': 'Virginia Postrel lays out the case for glamour as a life-shaping force, whether for good or for ill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The jury is still out on glamour. Some critics see it as the scourge of modern society, an illusory opiate doled out by a ruling elite to dazzle the masses into submission. For others glamour is a fragile and endangered species, the inspirational product of simpler times — before, say, a photograph of Scarlett Johansson tripping and falling could become, in the space of an hour, an Internet meme — as our tell-all society collapses the distance between us and our idols irrevocably.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '71', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08camhi-sub2/08camhi-sub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08camhi-sub2/08camhi-sub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 424}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08camhi-sub2/08camhi-sub2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 724, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08camhi-sub2/08camhi-sub2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 867, 'width': 1227, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08camhi-sub2/08camhi-sub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08camhi-sub2/08camhi-sub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Allure', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Allure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Camhi, Leslie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Postrel, Virginia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:17:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leslie Camhi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leslie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Camhi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cc56286-a6ee-50e6-b4c0-daef2ef83292', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cc56286-a6ee-50e6-b4c0-daef2ef83292'}, {'abstract': 'An illustrated overview of the main styles and production centers of Greek figurative pottery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/john-h-oakleys-greek-vase.html', 'snippet': 'An illustrated overview of the main styles and production centers of Greek figurative pottery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not so long ago, developing an informed appreciation for Greek vases, in particular fineware with painted scenes, was no easy matter for anyone aside from serious students and dedicated connoisseurs with access to the world’s museums. As for illustrated books, there wasn’t much between the affordable volumes of John Boardman’s Thames & Hudson paperback series, with their masterly text and plentiful but small and hard-to-see photographs in black and white, and expensive volumes with magnificent photos, mostly relegated to academic libraries. That’s all changed, largely because of the Internet, where high-resolution photographs of individual vases — on academic and curatorial websites with expert analysis, certainly, but also on the Flickr accounts of museumgoing tourists — let you zoom in on the tiny details in which much of the delight of Greek vase painting lies. But the lower cost of printing has also put beautifully printed large-format books within reach, and for readers getting interested in Greek vases for the first time, a coherent illustrated overview of the subject is invaluable and much more manageable on the page than on-screen.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '70', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08COATES/08COATES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08COATES/08COATES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 872}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08COATES/08COATES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 705, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08COATES/08COATES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1410, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08COATES/08COATES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08COATES/08COATES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08COATES/08COATES-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Classical Pottery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical Pottery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greek Civilization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oakley, John H', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:17:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Coates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b8df226-98b8-505d-90aa-0e5ee130d73f', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b8df226-98b8-505d-90aa-0e5ee130d73f'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Conlin offers a series of strolls through the history of Paris and London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/tales-of-two-cities-by-jonathan-conlin.html', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Conlin offers a series of strolls through the history of Paris and London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to frills and fashions, London defers to Paris. It was ever so. Jonathan Conlin, the American-born author of an admired history of London’s National Gallery, starts his series of sprightly strolls through these two great cities by imagining the journey of a “fashion doll,” a child-size mannequin that could be kitted out in the latest Paris style and brought back via Calais or Ostend for an astute dressmaker to use as her model when arraying London’s titled fashion lovers. The first such doll crossed the Channel — ready for the latest take on wimples, ladies? — in the 14th century. Prudently, the author confines his study in cultural crossings to the two centuries leading up to the Great War.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '69', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SEYMOURsub/08SEYMOURsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SEYMOURsub/08SEYMOURsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 735}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SEYMOURsub/08SEYMOURsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 837, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SEYMOURsub/08SEYMOURsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1673, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SEYMOURsub/08SEYMOURsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SEYMOURsub/08SEYMOURsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SEYMOURsub/08SEYMOURsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris-London', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paris-London', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conlin, Jonathan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:17:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miranda Seymour', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miranda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seymour', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c629f9f7-bf7f-5018-8933-25dcb25c6b07', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c629f9f7-bf7f-5018-8933-25dcb25c6b07'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of the puppeteer whose influence has hardly waned in the more than two decades since his death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/jim-henson-the-biography-by-brian-jay-jones.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of the puppeteer whose influence has hardly waned in the more than two decades since his death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not easy being a puppeteer. Consider the opening sequence of 1979’s “The Muppet Movie,” in which Kermit the Frog performed “The Rainbow Connection,” the \xadfollow-up to his breakout number, “Bein’ Green.” Kermit strummed his banjo while perched on a log in the middle of a swamp. The set had been carefully constructed on a studio back lot, but even fake swamps have real water. This required Jim Henson, Kermit’s creator and performer, to execute the complicated sequence submerged. As Kermit crooned, Henson lurked beneath the surface of the ersatz bayou, his lanky frame folded into a \xadcustom-made diving bell, his oxygen pumped in through a tube. To make his frog sing, the puppeteer had to become amphibious. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '68', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SWANSBURG/08SWANSBURG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 539, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08SWANSBURG/08SWANSBURG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 539}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '