of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/11a74798-1169-5ed9-aefa-5005ac47ab9e', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11a74798-1169-5ed9-aefa-5005ac47ab9e'}, {'abstract': 'Memoirs about two very different musical journeys.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/play-it-again-and-strings-attached.html', 'snippet': 'Memoirs about two very different musical journeys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seventeen pages into “Play It Again: An Amateur Against the Impossible,” Alan Rusbridger offers readers an implicit dare. Here the typeface changes from words to musical notes — not just any notes, but excerpts from Frédéric Chopin’s Ballade No. 1 in G minor, Op. 23, one of the most complex and challenging pieces in piano repertoire.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '72', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08HAFNER/08HAFNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 556, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08HAFNER/08HAFNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 556}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08HAFNER/08HAFNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08HAFNER/08HAFNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08HAFNER/08HAFNER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08HAFNER/08HAFNER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08HAFNER/08HAFNER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Music Lessons', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music Lessons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hafner, Katie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rusbridger, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipman, Joanne', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kupchynsky, Melanie', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:18:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Hafner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hafner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/315928ef-f98b-5530-95bd-42f2d6899fab', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/315928ef-f98b-5530-95bd-42f2d6899fab'}, {'abstract': 'A new book about Richard Wagner, who relentlessly recorded his daily life, and another about Bach, who left hardly any paper trail.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/bach-music-in-the-castle-of-heaven-and-richard-wagner-a-life-in-music.html', 'snippet': 'A new book about Richard Wagner, who relentlessly recorded his daily life, and another about Bach, who left hardly any paper trail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Jan. 1, 1869, the mother of two of Richard Wagner’s children, though not quite yet his wife, began keeping a diary. Though, calling the 14-year account that Cosima Wagner eventually produced a diary is like calling a battleship a boat. At the start of his idiosyncratic new book, “Richard Wagner: A Life in Music,” Martin Geck notes that Cosima’s journals ended up running nearly one million words. Full of mythologizing and after-the-fact edits, they give us, in conjunction with Wagner’s own copious writing and correspondence, an unprecedented opportunity: For no other cultural figure of his significance do we have a record of daily life so relentlessly extensive.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '73', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08woolfe/08woolfe-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 531, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08woolfe/08woolfe-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 531}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08woolfe/08woolfe-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 907, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08woolfe/08woolfe-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1328, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08woolfe/08woolfe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08woolfe/08woolfe-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08woolfe/08woolfe-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Composers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Composers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardiner, John Eliot', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Richard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bach, Johann Sebastian', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Geck, Martin', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spencer, Stewart', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:18:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b6d1c1a-1674-5697-9566-8087143d4f09', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b6d1c1a-1674-5697-9566-8087143d4f09'}, {'abstract': 'Sherill Tippins aims to tell the definitive story of New York’s Hotel Chelsea in “Inside the Dream Palace.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/inside-the-dream-palace-by-sherill-tippins.html', 'snippet': 'Sherill Tippins aims to tell the definitive story of New York’s Hotel Chelsea in “Inside the Dream Palace.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s a sad irony of New York life that over time, the fabled buildings and institutions that first attract us to the city fade into invisibility.” So begins Sherill Tippins’s thorough, if not groundbreaking, Hotel Chelsea history. For the past dozen or so years, the Chelsea has been raging against invisibility. Former guests and residents as different as Dee Dee Ramone, Abel Ferrara and Ethan Hawke have featured it in their work. Current residents have waged a very public battle with its recent owners. In anticipation of a potentially soul-killing renovation, two new books capture its lovably scuzzy spirit. One is an evocative coffee-table book by the photographer Victoria Cohen, HOTEL CHELSEA (Pointed Leaf Press, $95), which treats the oddly painted rooms’ quirky mix of old and new furniture, worn carpeting and garish bedspreads as something like sacred relics. The other is Tippins’s doorstop-hefty \xad“Inside the Dream Palace,” which aspires to tell the “definitive story.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '84', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08CALHOUNsub/08CALHOUNsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 305, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08CALHOUNsub/08CALHOUNsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 305}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08CALHOUNsub/08CALHOUNsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08CALHOUNsub/08CALHOUNsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1039, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08CALHOUNsub/08CALHOUNsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08CALHOUNsub/08CALHOUNsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/08CALHOUNsub/08CALHOUNsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Chelsea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Chelsea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tippins, Sherill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hotel Chelsea (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Victoria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T16:19:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ada Calhoun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calhoun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/68afe681-8e07-5afc-9d44-2b2dffbac909', 'word_count': 1153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68afe681-8e07-5afc-9d44-2b2dffbac909'}, {'abstract': 'The first of a projected three-volume biography of the Fab Four.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/tune-in-the-beatles-all-these-years-volume-1-by-mark-lewisohn.html', 'snippet': 'The first of a projected three-volume biography of the Fab Four.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Approaches to retelling the Beatles’ story slice in two distinct directions: narrow or wide. Some authors choose a single figure and bore down deep, which has brought the count of Paul McCartney life stories to at least 10, with more in the pipeline. Others frame the narrative from more expansive angles, weaving in the era’s social texture, politics and cultural context (see Devin McKinney’s shrewd “Magic Circles” from 2003 or Jonathan Gould’s peerless “Can’t Buy Me Love” of 2007).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208RILEY/1208RILEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208RILEY/1208RILEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208RILEY/1208RILEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 606, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208RILEY/1208RILEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1211, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208RILEY/1208RILEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/08/books/review/1208RILEY/1208RILEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit':