organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c72327f-e16c-5f41-aca4-10e270cccf38', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c72327f-e16c-5f41-aca4-10e270cccf38'}, {'abstract': 'In two short novels by Susan Hill, youthful misbehavior receives its just punishment, and then some.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/books/review/the-small-hand-and-dolly-by-susan-hill.html', 'snippet': 'In two short novels by Susan Hill, youthful misbehavior receives its just punishment, and then some.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We blot out bad things from our minds and especially when they have been bad things we ourselves have done, in childhood perhaps most of all.” So says the narrator of “Dolly,” the second of two short novels by Susan Hill in which bad things done in childhood refuse, despite grown-up minds’ best efforts, to stay blotted. Nasty stuff of this sort may resurface after years of psychoanalysis or — less therapeutically if more efficiently — in the course of a compact, elegantly constructed ghost story, which happens to be one of Hill’s specialties. Her nerve-\xadshaking 1983 novel “The Woman in Black” is a modern classic of the genre, in the whispery manner of M. R. James and Robert Aickman. (It was made into a movie, unmemorably, last year; a stage adaptation has been running in London since 1988.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/books/review/01rafferty-sub/01rafferty-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 572, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/06/books/review/01rafferty-sub/01rafferty-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 572}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/books/review/01r