 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T17:53:20+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/0e2fee94-a67b-5fa4-803f-db8ac2d16847', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/0e2fee94-a67b-5fa4-803f-db8ac2d16847'}, {'abstract': 'The director Scott Cooper explains how he wrote “Out of the Furnace” for Christian Bale, but chose to forgo rehearsals.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/06/no-rehearsals-for-a-prestige-cast/', 'snippet': 'The director Scott Cooper explains how he wrote “Out of the Furnace” for Christian Bale, but chose to forgo rehearsals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In terms of Oscar nominees and winners per frame, the ensemble films “August: Osage County” and “American Hustle” may be tough to beat this season. (Honorable mention: “Prisoners,” starring Hugh Jackman, Jake Gyllenhaal, Melissa Leo and Paul Dano.) But “Out of the Furnace,” from the writer-director Scott Cooper (whose “Crazy Heart” earned two Oscars, including a long-overdue statue for Jeff Bridges) and starring Christian Bale (Oscar winner), Casey Affleck (nominee), Woody Harrelson (nominee) and Forest Whitaker (winner) makes a strong showing. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/cooper/cooper-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/cooper/cooper-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/06/arts/cooper/cooper-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '