: 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/bb9ed595-eddb-537e-a7b7-1caa17b11d2d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/bb9ed595-eddb-537e-a7b7-1caa17b11d2d'}, {'abstract': 'The cause of Kawasaki disease is still unknown, but the thinking these days is that it is an unusual immune response to a probable viral infection in children who have some kind of genetic predisposition to it.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/06/think-like-a-doctor-eyes-solved/', 'snippet': 'The cause of Kawasaki disease is still unknown, but the thinking these days is that it is an unusual immune response to a probable viral infection in children who have some kind of genetic predisposition to it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday, we challenged Well readers to figure out why a 9-year-old boy had nearly a week of a waxing and waning fever and swollen glands. Turns out, it was not much of a challenge for many of our readers. A whopping 50 percent of those who responded recognized this disease as …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 400, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 400, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Think Like a Doctor: Eyes Solved', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eyes and Eyesight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T19:38:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Sanders, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8cf60d6-39fb-5b29-9fbc-cb6933bd4971', 'word_count': 1289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8cf60d6-39fb-5b29-9fbc-cb6933bd4971'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/06/the-gun-report-december-6-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': '* The House of Representatives overwhelmingly voted to extend the Undetectable Firearms Act, which makes it illegal to manufacture, possess or sell guns that lack material that can set off a metal detector—but didn’t amend the law to require permanent metal inserts in plastic guns. As the law stands, gun manufactures abide by an honor system whereby they promise to keep enough metal in a weapon to set off alarms. The N.R.A. has so far stayed silent on the measure’s bipartisan renewal. The bill, sponsored by North Carolina Republican Howard Coble, now heads to the Senate, where Democratic lawmakers are considering revisions to deal with emerging technology, like 3D printed guns.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: December 6, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-06T19:43:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By JENNIFER MASCIA', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MASCIA', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bccf8a5-705e-5042-84f8-982ae1b4c089', 'word_count': 1505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bccf8a5-705e-5042-84f8-982ae1b4c089'}, {'abstract': 'Despite trying times and family grief, they made a match.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/fashion/weddings/after-trying-times-at-last-something-to-celebrate.html', 'snippet': 'Despite trying times and family grief, they made a match.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marni Galison was single and looking — but not for herself.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/fashion/08VOWS1_SPAN/08vows01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/08/fashion/08VOWS1_SPAN/08vows01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/08/fashion/08VOWS1_SPAN/08vows01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_na