'uri': 'nyt://article/4c3dc30b-d753-52c0-82ba-410598e01a6b'}, {'abstract': 'Operators nearing the area, just north of Spuyten Duyvil station, will be warned of a reduced speed limit, and the train will brake automatically.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/09/nyregion/senators-propose-cameras-inside-cabs-of-trains-post-metro-north-accident.html', 'snippet': 'Operators nearing the area, just north of Spuyten Duyvil station, will be warned of a reduced speed limit, and the train will brake automatically.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One week after a Metro-North train derailed in the Bronx, killing four people and injuring scores of others, the Metropolitan Transportation Authority said on Sunday night that it had upgraded its signal network to warn operators as they approach the sharply curving section of track where the crash occurred.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Signals Upgraded for Curve in Track Where Metro-North Crash Occurred', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Signals Upgraded For Curve In Track', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroad Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spuyten Duyvil (Bronx, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metro-North Commuter Railroad', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cameras', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Driver Distraction and Fatigue', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumer, Charles E', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blumenthal, Richard', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rockefeller, William Jr', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York Metropolitan Area', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-09T02:04:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer and Vivian Yee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Vivian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77585eec-9d2e-502d-beca-46262cbb8fad', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77585eec-9d2e-502d-beca-46262cbb8fad'}, {'abstract': 'A Republican case for a $1 trillion stimulus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/09/opinion/saving-the-fed-from-itself.html', 'snippet': 'A Republican case for a $1 trillion stimulus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAMBRIDGE, Mass. — THE Federal Reserve is pursuing a very risky monetary policy. Its leaders — the departing chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, and the vice chairwoman, Janet L. Yellen, whom President Obama has nominated to succeed him — are correct that the American economy needs more stimulus, and they believe that the central bank, because of political paralysis, is the only game in town. But if Congress and the Obama administration could agree on a fiscal stimulus that goes beyond a short-term budget deal, the Fed would not have to take such risks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saving the Fed From Itself', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saving The Fed From Itself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-09T02:05:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin S. Feldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Feldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/49b88e1d-e14f-5499-87b6-739551124a39', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49b88e1d-e14f-5499-87b6-739551124a39'}, {'abstract': 'China and Britain both underscore how transparency, however painful in the moment, allows democracy, business and the citizenry to thrive in the long run.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/09/business/media/where-freedom-of-the-press-is-muffled.html', 'snippet': 'China and Britain both underscore how transparency, however painful in the moment, allows democracy, business and the citizenry to thrive in the long run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In China on Thursday, Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr. spoke plainly about the role of a free press in a democratic society.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/timestopics/sub-david-carr/sub-david-carr-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/timestopics/sub-david-carr/sub-david-carr-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/timestopics/sub-david-carr/sub-david-carr-jumbo.jpg', '