ece on a point I’ve also tried to make: the irrelevance of “helicopter money”, and in particular the irrelevance of the decision to finance budget deficits by printing money as opposed to selling bonds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Helicopters Don’t Help (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-09T13:14:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/260e8f5b-ba9d-57ee-bd73-98696e5c2aaf', 'word_count': 544, 'uri': 'nyt://article/260e8f5b-ba9d-57ee-bd73-98696e5c2aaf'}, {'abstract': 'Los Angeles critics picked “Gravity” and “Her” as best film while their Boston counterparts went with “12 Years a Slave.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/09/critics-in-l-a-and-boston-split-over-years-best/', 'snippet': 'Los Angeles critics picked “Gravity” and “Her” as best film while their Boston counterparts went with “12 Years a Slave.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pool of Oscar hopefuls continues to grow, as evidenced by the number of ties in the Los Angeles Film Critics Association’s year-end choices, announced Sunday. “Gravity” and “Her” both took home the best film award; Cate Blanchett (“Blue Jasmine”) and Adèle Exarchopoulos (“Blue Is the Warmest Color”) tied for best actress; and James Franco (“Spring Breakers”) and Jared Leto (“Dallas Buyers Club”) will have to share the prize for best supporting actor. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics in L.A. and Boston Split Over Year’s Best', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_