 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/512a0048-fc4a-5553-914b-8266f40d647c', 'word_count': 622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/512a0048-fc4a-5553-914b-8266f40d647c'}, {'abstract': '“The (Curious Case of the) Watson Intelligence” weaves love and machines into its plot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/10/theater/reviews/the-curious-case-of-the-watson-intelligence.html', 'snippet': '“The (Curious Case of the) Watson Intelligence” weaves love and machines into its plot.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The (Curious Case of the) Watson Intelligence” probably seemed like a real dazzler when it was in the mind of its author, the playwright Madeleine George. But like many newfangled inventions that seem spectacular in blueprint form — good luck with those creepy-looking drones, Amazon! — the play now chugging across the stage at Playwrights Horizons too often sputters and stalls, falling victim to its own grand ambitions. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/10WATSON_SPAN/10WATSON_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/10WATSON_SPAN/10WATSON_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 521}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/10WATSON_SPAN/10WATSON_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 889, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/10WATSON_SPAN/10WATSON_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1778, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/10WATSON_SPAN/10WATSON_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/10WATSON_SPAN/10WATSON_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/10WATSON_SPAN/10WATSON_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smarts and Feelings, Vying for Attention', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smarts and Feelings, Vying for Attention', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George, Madeleine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silverman, Leigh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conlee, John Ellison', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Costabile, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quaid, Amanda', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-10T03:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c88b99b9-cca2-5244-98ef-13978c6a0ef0', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c88b99b9-cca2-5244-98ef-13978c6a0ef0'}, {'abstract': 'A sweet puzzle by Bill Thompson.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/09/higher-calling/', 'snippet': 'A sweet puzzle by Bill Thompson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUESDAY’S PUZZLE — There was a brief time in my household where I had to lock up the MAGIC MARKERs. The Girl was 3 years old, and she was so enamored of the colors and what she could do with them that it became clear to me that she needed close supervision. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Higher Calling', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-10T03:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By DEB AMLEN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'AMLEN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67702d4b-5e2e-5bc5-b00c-671926d140a1', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67702d4b-5e2e-5bc5-b00c-671926d140a1'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the elite California university raises broader questions about education and the middle class.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/10/opinion/nocera-the-berkeley-model.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the elite California university raises broader questions about education and the middle class.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fred Wiseman is the grand old man of documentary filmmaking. Over the course of his long career, he has made 41 movies, the vast majority of them documentaries. They tend to share certain traits. They are often quite long. They rely entirely on dialogue: Wiseman never uses narration in his documentaries. And they often focus on institutions. Wiseman has done films about a mental hospital (“Titicut Follies”), a police precinct (“Law and Order”), and a high school (“High School”). Some of his films have the force of an exposé; others impart the documentary equivalent of a big wet kiss.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Berkeley Model', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Berkeley Model', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nocera, Joseph', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiseman, Frederick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'At Berkeley (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California, Berkeley', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tuition', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-10T03:02:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b17b30bb-e2be-53a2-aab5-05e9ad01d0d7', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b17b30bb-e2be-53a2-aab5-05e9ad01d0d7'}, {'abstract': 'Overaggressive parents hollering from the stands have become a cliché, but some Kentucky moms have turned parental cheering into an art form — and, they hope, a business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/10/sports/real-basketball-moms-of-kentucky.html', 'snippet': 'Overaggressive parents hollering from the stands have become a cliché, but some Kentucky moms have turned parental cheering into an art form — and, they hope, a business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOUISVILLE, Ky. — Michelle Green brings a billboard-size poster of her son’s head to his basketball games and has been known to cheer so aggressively that she has been thrown out of the gym. Susie Walker-Byrd has switched her son’s high school three times to accommodate his basketball dreams. Carmelita Clay is convinced that her son should be on national television because of his basketball skills. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/sports/MOMS/MOMS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/10/sports/MOMS/MOMS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/sports/MOMS/MOMS-jumbo