', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91aa23b1-ff76-5f3d-b081-474cd4abde53', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91aa23b1-ff76-5f3d-b081-474cd4abde53'}, {'abstract': 'As a gesture of holiday cheer and economic good will, the cult boutique in SoHo has curated an appealing collection of stocking stuffers sourced from Greece.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/10/in-store-for-the-new-york-design-shop-kiosk-its-good-to-be-greek/', 'snippet': 'As a gesture of holiday cheer and economic good will, the cult boutique in SoHo has curated an appealing collection of stocking stuffers sourced from Greece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hidden in a gritty upstairs loft amid SoHo’s multitude of megastores, Kiosk — the Spring Street object boutique founded in 2005 by the husband-and-wife team Marco ter Haar Romeny and Alisa Grifo — defiantly embraces the small-scale and the local. The couple travels through a different country each season to source what they call, with deadpan accuracy, “things from places.” And while their focus is on inexpensive everyday trinkets rather than contemporary design, the duo’s eye for clever packaging and typography (Grifo, a set designer, has worked at the Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum and Metropolis magazine) helps them gather the sort of whimsical, accidentally chic souvenirs you wish you’d brought back from Provence or Portugal yourself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Store | For the New York Design Shop Kiosk, It’s Good to Be Greek', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grifo, Alisa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-10T17:41:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5feccd3b-b0af-5509-82b3-5965a669aeb0', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5feccd3b-b0af-5509-82b3-5965a669aeb0'}, {'abstract': 'The director of “Fruitvale Station” checked whether the film was working by looking at the individual character arc of the protagonist’s girlfriend or mother.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/10/fruitvale-station-re-edited/', 'snippet': 'The director of “Fruitvale Station” checked whether the film was working by looking at the individual character arc of the protagonist’s girlfriend or mother.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s already been a sweet awards ride for “Fruitvale Station,” a hit at Sundance that has gone on to collect trophies from the Gothams, the New York Film Critics Circle, the National Board of Review and a best-of-the-year mention from the American Film Institute. It’s the story of Oscar Grant III, who was killed by a transit officer in Oakland, Calif., in 2009. The film’s star, Michael B. Jordan,  spoke with Oprah Winfrey recently, and he and the film’s 27-year-old director, Ryan Coogler, were named to Time magazine’s list of young people changing the world. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/ryancoogler/ryancoogler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1196, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/10/arts/ryancoogler/ryancoogler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600