ental Illness, Not Irony', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Understanding Suicide: Mental Illness, Not Irony', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hecht, Jennifer Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-10T23:42:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b85981d-5f7a-546e-8891-09c694dfeb5d', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b85981d-5f7a-546e-8891-09c694dfeb5d'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a Sunday Review essay by Charles McGrath, “Caution: Reading Can Be Hazardous.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/opinion/fiction-as-a-religion.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a Sunday Review essay by Charles McGrath, “Caution: Reading Can Be Hazardous.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction as a Religion', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction as a Religion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-10T23:43:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/372a7659-b70f-5cdc-8675-89aec2df6c74', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/372a7659-b70f-5cdc-8675-89aec2df6c74'}, {'abstract': 'To play on the perception that contemporary art has been completely commoditized, some clever young artists are using currency as a raw material.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/10/threes-a-trend-art-thats-all-about-the-money-literally/', 'snippet': 'To play on the perception that contemporary art has been completely commoditized, some clever young artists are using currency as a raw material.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On paper, art is a lot like money. The value of an art object has almost nothing to do with the time required to make it, and even less to do with its materials; if counterfeit, its worth is nil. So it’s no wonder that some physical artists in a disembodied world are — like Warhol in the plastic age or Rihanna in “Pour It Up” — all about the benjamins. At this year’s Art Basel in Miami Beach, Scott Campbell (OHWOW Gallery) showed sculptures built with greenbacks and Hugh Scott-Douglas (Blum & Poe) manipulated foreign currency in a set of his signature magnified scans. Meanwhile, over at NADA, Robert Wechsler (China Art Objects Galleries) exhibited perfectly symmetrical cubes constructed from spare change. In a time of relentless secondary-market speculation that turns young artists into stocks just waiting to be cashed in, it’s almost as if the artists themselves are turning around and saying: “Happy now?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Three’s a Trend | Art That’s All About the Money … Literally', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami Beach (Fla)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-10T23:44:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Nicole Prickett', 'person': 