, {'abstract': 'Seven Sunni construction workers were killed by unidentified gunmen who fired on them from a speeding S.U.V. in Abara, a majority-Sunni village in Diyala Province, police and medical officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/world/middleeast/iraq.html', 'snippet': 'Seven Sunni construction workers were killed by unidentified gunmen who fired on them from a speeding S.U.V. in Abara, a majority-Sunni village in Diyala Province, police and medical officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Seven Sunni construction workers were killed by unidentified gunmen who fired on them from a speeding S.U.V. on Tuesday morning in Abara, a majority-Sunni village in Diyala Province, police and medical officials said. When their families took the bodies to a ritual washing hall, a bomb exploded, killing six family members, two women and four men. That evening, a suicide bomber targeted a Shiite shrine in Diyala, killing 12 Shiites. “Al Qaeda has succeeded in its strategy,” said Haider al-Musawi, a security expert and former army officer. “Now Sunnis think that the Shiites are killing them, and the Shiites think that the Sunnis killed them.” He said the situation could become worse than Syria barring an immediate solution, which would be “a miracle.” The 25 were among 31 people killed in Iraq on Tuesday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iraq: 13 Sunnis Killed, Then 12 Shiites', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: 13 Sunnis Killed, Then 12 Shiites', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adnan, Duraid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunni Muslims', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T02:04:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Duraid Adnan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duraid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adnan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0080c4c5-4e93-510f-a551-a889a2802495', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0080c4c5-4e93-510f-a551-a889a2802495'}, {'abstract': 'The N.B.A. issued 21 fines and suspensions worth over $602,000 in November, which matched the total from the first full calendar month of the previous three seasons combined.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/sports/basketball/nba-players-are-paying-by-the-rules.html', 'snippet': 'The N.B.A. issued 21 fines and suspensions worth over $602,000 in November, which matched the total from the first full calendar month of the previous three seasons combined.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not all basketball players can dunk. Not every coach can take his team to the playoffs. But anyone in the N.B.A. can be fined — and lately, it seems, almost everyone has.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/11/sports/SUB-FINES-ILLO/SUB-FINES-ILLO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/11/sports/SUB-FINES-ILLO/SUB-FINES-ILLO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 430}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/11/sports/SUB-FINES-ILLO/SUB-FINES-ILLO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur