ia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7f9e5d6-8ae6-5b06-8c27-3a2f3b919511', 'word_count': 1685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7f9e5d6-8ae6-5b06-8c27-3a2f3b919511'}, {'abstract': 'The director of “American Hustle” on redirecting his own career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/15/magazine/david-o-russell-in-conversation.html', 'snippet': 'The director of “American Hustle” on redirecting his own career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You could argue that, with his new film, “American Hustle,” coming on the heels of “The Fighter” and “Silver Linings Playbook,” David O. Russell has put together the best trifecta of any active American filmmaker. “The Fighter” earned seven Oscar nominations; “Silver Linings” received eight; and “American Hustle” was just named the year’s best film by the New York Film Critics Circle. Early in his career, Russell developed a reputation as a brilliant but difficult director; George Clooney said the two actually came to blows on the set of Russell’s third film, “Three Kings,” and later a YouTube video circulated of Lily Tomlin and Russell in a full-throated, expletive-laced shouting match on the set of his fourth film, “I Heart Huckabees.” Whether\xad the characterization was fair, Russell has since undergone a personal, artistic and, ultimately, professional transformation. He regards “Hustle,” a highly fictionalized version of the Abscam scandal of the late ’70s and early ’80s, as the third in a loose trilogy of films about ordinary people trying to live passionate lives. It’s a journey he has had some experience with firsthand.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/magazine/15russell1/15russell1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/15/magazine/15russell1/15russell1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/magazine/15russell1/15russell1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/magazine/15russell1/15russell1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1639, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/magazine/15russell1/15russell1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/15/magazine/15russell1/15russell1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/magazine/15russell1/15russell1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'David O. Russell: In Conversation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Mr. Overthinking Things, How About Really, Really Just Do It as Good as You Can From Your Heart. Can You Do That?’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gabler, Neal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Russell, David O', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACT