ndon Interbank Offered Rate)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diamond, Robert E Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Boris', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citi Bike', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T12:36:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier and Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f294f2d-66bd-5c69-a435-109042a40bff', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f294f2d-66bd-5c69-a435-109042a40bff'}, {'abstract': 'Supply, not demand.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/potential-misunderstandings/', 'snippet': 'Supply, not demand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Izabella Kaminska reports on a “strongly worded” riposte to the secular stagnation hypothesis, in which analysts at Independent Strategy declare that we don’t have inadequate demand, we have debt causing the economy to grow above potential. Unfortunately, the paper itself isn’t available, only Kaminska’s description, so I can’t see how strong the wording is. If her description is accurate, however, some other stuff is … not so strong. For it seems that like so many writers in this area, the authors don’t understand the meaning of potential output.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Potential Misunderstandings', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T12:48:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23b71114-f3d0-555e-89e0-2e65d81049fa', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23b71114-f3d0-555e-89e0-2e65d81049fa'}, {'abstract': 'Our outsourced servant class.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/upstairs-downstairs-outside/', 'snippet': 'Our outsourced servant class.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Via Mark Thoma, David Cay Johnston has a great piece noting that today’s service economy is in many ways like the Edwardian-era economy in which a small number of wealthy people employed a large number of servants — except that we tend to outsource the service, relying on restaurants and cleaning services instead of cooks and maids. And our outsourced servants are, he notes, arguably paid and treated worse than the in-house servants of the past, even in absolute terms — let alone relative to per capita GDP.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Upstairs, Downstairs, Outside', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T12:55:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90b117ff-df73-595f-babd-d3214f56b624', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90b117ff-df73-595f-babd-d3214f56b624'}, {'abstract': 'Political compromises and economic help from the European Union could ease the crisis that continues to deepen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/12/opinion/a-dangerously-divided-ukraine.html', 'snippet': 'Political compromises and economic help from the European Union could ease the crisis that continues to deepen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ukraine’s president, Viktor Yanukovich, took a tentative step toward defusing his country’s political turmoil earlier this week by convening a “round table” with former Ukrainian presidents to discuss possible responses to the protests engulfing the capital, Kiev. But he promptly negated any value in that gesture by ordering security police to clear the demonstrators’ encampment in Independence Square early Wednesday morning. Though the police appeared to pull back by midday, the confrontation plunges Ukraine deeper into crisis. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Dangerously Divided Ukraine', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Dangerously Divided Ukraine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yanukovich, Viktor F', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T12:58:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b765065-3dc6-5a0d-bb36-4ff742ce1cc8', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://articl