None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/942b5c42-63e3-5470-9148-76e8ced08628', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/942b5c42-63e3-5470-9148-76e8ced08628'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama may want to look back at his predecessors’ political fortunes in their sixth year in office. Then he may want to close his eyes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/12/us/politics/obama-midterm-election.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama may want to look back at his predecessors’ political fortunes in their sixth year in office. Then he may want to close his eyes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — History says President Obama should brace for another round of midterm election losses next year — and be grateful for the opportunity.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When a Second-Term President Nears the Midterm Shoals', 'kicker': 'Political Memo', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When a Second-Term President Nears the Midterm Shoals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harwood, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T16:23:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Harwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d6ec76d-0b0a-56a8-bf4a-66219feac038', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d6ec76d-0b0a-56a8-bf4a-66219feac038'}, {'abstract': 'Films from Israel and the Palestinian territories are both thrillers about an Israeli intelligence agent and a Palestinian informer.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/a-geopolitical-twist-in-the-foreign-film-race/', 'snippet': 'Films from Israel and the Palestinian territories are both thrillers about an Israeli intelligence agent and a Palestinian informer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every now and then, the Oscar competition for best foreign-language film is enlivened when it turns out that filmmakers from different places have tackled the same subject, unaware that someone else had the same idea. That’s the case this year, but with an additional geopolitical twist: the director of one of the films involved is an Israeli, and the director of the other is Palestinian.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Geopolitical Twist in the Foreign Film Race', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABU-ASSAD, HANY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T16:30:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bd61b7d-e3ea-5bc2-82cc-7d1bc7c239f7', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bd61b7d-e3ea-5bc2-82cc-7d1bc7c239f7'}, {'abstract': 'The Palestinian director Hany Abu-Assad explains how incidents during the making of a previous film led to “Omar,” about a Palestinian informant and Israeli intelligence.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/hany-abu-assad-on-omar-and-strange-coincidences/', 'snippet': 'The Palestinian director Hany Abu-Assad explains how incidents during the making of a previous film led to “Omar,” about a Palestinian informant and Israeli intelligence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2006, the Palestinian director Hany Abu-Assad’s drama “Paradise Now,” about two young Palestinian men planning a suicide attack in Israel, won the Golden Globe for best foreign-language film and was also nominated for an Oscar in that category. Now, after an unhappy interlude in which he made a Hollywood action film called “The Courier,” Mr. Abu-Assad is back in the thick of awards season with “Omar,” which looks again at the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, but from a different angle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hany Abu-Assad on ‘Omar’ and Strange Coincidences', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T16:35:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49958f38-2564-5c5b-b1ec-36268cc57846', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49958f38-2564-5c5b-b1ec-36268cc57846'}, {'abstract': 'The Israeli director Yuval Adler came to film in a roundabout way that includes a doctorate in philosophy.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/yuval-adler-on-bethlehem-and-heidegger/', 'snippet': 'The Israeli director Yuval Adler came to film in a roundabout way that includes a doctorate in philosophy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 4:54 p.m. | Before beginning a career in film, Yuval Adler, the director of “Bethlehem,” Israel’s submission for the foreign-language Oscar, worked in a lot of different fields. After earning a degree in mathematics in Tel Aviv, he was a numbers cruncher in New York “for some guy who was doing hedge fund stuff,” while simultaneously trying to develop himself as an artist, living in a studio in Williamsburg, Brooklyn. He also earned a Ph.D in philosophy from Columbia University, writing his dissertation on finger-pointing as an example of a linguistic phenomenon called indexicality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Yuval Adler on ‘Bethlehem’ and Heidegger', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T16:35:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16a7be1e-6182-5344-9713-23e95c3c96d5', 'word_count': 1265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16a7be1e-6182-5344-9713-23e95c3c96d5'}, {'abstract': 'Uri Shulevitz’s “Dusk” follows a little boy and his grandfather on their afternoon walk through the streets of New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/12/books/dusk-by-uri-shulevitz.html', 'snippet': 'Uri Shulevitz’s “Dusk” follows a little boy and his grandfather on their afternoon walk through the streets of New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the interval between Hanukkah and Christmas, as we head toward the winter solstice, light — from\xa0the sun,\xa0streetlamps, or\xa0candles — seems more precious than ever. In his new picture book, “Dusk,” the Warsaw-born author and illustrator Uri Shulevitz follows a little boy and his grandfather on their afternoon walk through the streets of New York, following the shifting lights of the short day as they go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/12/books/12childrens-1/12childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 270, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/12/books/12childrens-1/12childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 270}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/12/books/12childrens-1/12childrens-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 461, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/12/books/12childrens-1/12childrens-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/12/books/12childrens-1/12childrens-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/12/books/12childrens-1/12childrens-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/12/books/12childrens-1/12childrens-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lighting Up the Night', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shulevitz, Uri', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-11T16:37:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Harrison Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Harrison', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/46663de4-d389-5086-9a2e-912732158261', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46663de4-d389-5086-9a2e-912732158261'}, {'abstract': 'Santa is 100 percent not licensed to produce iPads or any brand-name electric devices — and with good reason.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/11/7-reasons-not-to-buy-your-child-an-ipad-for-christmas/', 'snippet': 'Santa is 100 percent not licensed to produce iPads or any brand-name electric devices — and with good reason.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to Christmas, I’m a sucker for the “wow” gift (and if anyone has any ideas for my 8-year-old daughter, let me know). I want to surprise and delight as much as anyone (and as an interfaith family, we alternate doing the big gifts for Hanukkah or for Christmas — the timing means it’s Christmas this year). But there’s one big gift I won’t go for: the tablet computer in any form, and that includes the game-and-video playing Kindle Fire. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/11/style/motherlode-dolls-reading/motherl