h Africa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Funerals and Memorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-12T12:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1913588-278b-5a66-b672-974a3d5731ce', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1913588-278b-5a66-b672-974a3d5731ce'}, {'abstract': 'The man, accused by sign-language experts of being an impostor, said in interviews that he suffered from schizophrenia and had a tendency to violence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/13/world/africa/mandela-memorial-interpreter.html', 'snippet': 'The man, accused by sign-language experts of being an impostor, said in interviews that he suffered from schizophrenia and had a tendency to violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PRETORIA, South Africa — A man accused of masquerading as a sign-language interpreter during Nelson Mandela’s memorial service, after he stood beside President Obama and other global dignitaries and gesticulated in gibberish, told interviewers on Thursday that he was a violence-prone schizophrenic and had seen angels descending in the stadium where the event was held.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/12/world/interpreter/interpreter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 299, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/12/world/interpreter/interpreter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 299}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cre