 grip oil has had on Mexico’s soul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CIUDAD DEL CARMEN, Mexico — Every gas station in Mexico is stamped with the green-and-white logo of the state-owned oil monopoly, the economic lifeblood of the government. Oil Expropriation Day, commemorating the day Mexico seized control of the industry from foreign companies in 1938, is celebrated with speeches and even parades in some towns. An old song, “The Oil Worker Hymn,” credits oil with “saving our fatherland.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico’s Pride, Oil, May Be Opened to Outsiders', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico’s Pride, Oil, May Be Opened to Outsiders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Archibold, Randal C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pena Nieto, Enrique', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pemex', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privatization', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-12T19:03:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold and Elisabeth Malkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'orga