isible Child,” was 15 months in the making. At 28,738 words, and accompanied by affecting images by Ruth Fremson, a staff photographer, this story of a homeless girl in Brooklyn is the largest investigative project that The Times has run all at once.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "‘Invisible Child': Behind the Scenes, Before and After", 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-12T22:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7127ca8-2a0e-56b0-bb03-336f171d8752', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7127ca8-2a0e-56b0-bb03-336f171d8752'}, {'abstract': 'As her “Drawing Which Makes Itself” show at the Museum of Modern Art makes clear, Dorothea Rockburne’s art has always been informed by geometrics and proportions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/13/arts/design/dorothea-rockburnes-drawings-at-the-museum-of-modern-art.html', 'snippet': 'As her “Drawing Which Makes Itself” show at the Museum of Modern Art makes clear, Dorothea Rockburne’s art has always been informed by geometrics and proportions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Spend an afternoon dipping in and out of galleries on the Lower East Side, and you are likely to encounter many examples of geometric abstraction — much of it offhand and whimsical. But if you want to see the work of an artist who cares deeply about geometry, pay a visit to Dorothea Rockburne’s austere, bracing exhibition of drawings at the Museum of Modern Art. The difference is instructive.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13ROCKBURNE1_SPAN/ROCKBURNE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13ROCKBURNE1_SPAN/ROCKBURNE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13ROCKBURNE1_SPAN/ROCKBURNE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13ROCKBURNE1_SPAN/ROCKBURNE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1343, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13ROCKBURNE1_SPAN/ROCKBURNE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13ROCKBURNE1_SPAN/ROCKBURNE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13ROCKBURNE1_SPAN/ROCKBURNE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mathematical Ratios, Papered, Folded and Cut', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mathematical Ratios, Papered, Folded and Cut', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenberg, Karen', '