', 'snippet': '“Damage Control” at the Hirshhorn explores artists’ responses to humanity’s untrammeled capacity for violence in the postwar period.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Air raid drills were the norm for American grade school kids in the Cold War 1950s. If you were in class, and the alarm went off, you got under your desk with your hands on your head. How was that supposed to save you from bombs? You didn’t know. You just hoped it would.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13DAMAGES_SPAN/13DAMAGES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13DAMAGES_SPAN/13DAMAGES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13DAMAGES_SPAN/13DAMAGES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13DAMAGES_SPAN/13DAMAGES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1332, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13DAMAGES_SPAN/13DAMAGES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13DAMAGES_SPAN/13DAMAGES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13DAMAGES_SPAN/13DAMAGES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLa