rks', 'value': 'Narco Cultura (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-12T23:22:58+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'Margaret Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/38b6439b-b89b-57f4-a26e-633eecc40646', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/38b6439b-b89b-57f4-a26e-633eecc40646'}, {'abstract': 'Sondra Radvanovsky sang the title role in Luc Bondy’s bleak version of “Tosca” at the Metropolitan Opera.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/13/arts/music/tosca-at-the-metropolitan-opera.html', 'snippet': 'Sondra Radvanovsky sang the title role in Luc Bondy’s bleak version of “Tosca” at the Metropolitan Opera.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some opera productions are defined by what’s not there. Luc Bondy’s bleak “Tosca” has been part of the Metropolitan Opera’s repertory since 2009, when it opened the season to lusty booing. That was due in great part to the grim sets, which have none of the historical detail of the Franco Zeffirelli production. Only bare brick walls and what appear to be Ikea folding chairs offer respite for a viewer tired of puzzling over that Lucian Freud painting that has somehow found its way into the church, perhaps dumped there by Napoleonic troops after an art looting expedition into the future. Also missing in Mr. Bondy’s “Tosca” are the crucifix and candles she places on and around the body of the villainous Scarpia after she has stabbed him to death.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13TOSCA/13TOSCA-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name