{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-12T23:26:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b76cbb7b-39a3-5675-a6c3-8d050d1c6e98', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b76cbb7b-39a3-5675-a6c3-8d050d1c6e98'}, {'abstract': '“What’s in a Name?,” directed by Matthieu Delaporte and Alexandre de la Patellière, centers on a fractious dinner party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/13/movies/whats-in-a-name-a-farce-about-a-fractious-evening.html', 'snippet': '“What’s in a Name?,” directed by Matthieu Delaporte and Alexandre de la Patellière, centers on a fractious dinner party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The well-lubricated dinner party that goes haywire has been a standard platform for nasty domestic comedy ever since the days of Georges Feydeau. In “What’s In a Name?,” an uproarious French farce, the catalyst for the flinging of mud isn’t booze but a practical joke that is carried so far it rattles skeletons. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13WHATSINANAME_SPAN/WHATS-INANAME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13WHATSINANAME_SPAN/WHATS-INANAME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/13/arts/13WHATSINANAME_SPAN/WHATS-INANAME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width'