'nyt://article/984641d1-49fe-554e-a1e6-91a2496790f3', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/984641d1-49fe-554e-a1e6-91a2496790f3'}, {'abstract': 'Though some games, like Tomb Raider, offer heroines, some gamers are personally filling a gap by creating strong female characters of their own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/15/arts/video-games/chris-suellentrop-on-the-year-in-video-games.html', 'snippet': 'Though some games, like Tomb Raider, offer heroines, some gamers are personally filling a gap by creating strong female characters of their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The men behind some of the most popular and celebrated video games in the medium’s not-as-short-as-you-think history concede that they haven’t spent an enormous amount of time pondering the portrayal of women in their work. “Seemingly not as much as I should have” was the sheepish acknowledgment Sam Houser, a founder of Rockstar Games, gave me during a long interview in Manhattan in September about Grand Theft Auto V, his newest game.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/arts/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/15/arts/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/arts/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/15/arts/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN/15SUELLENTROP1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo'