ign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'Bethany Matta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bethany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Matta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/8ed24663-ea17-52b7-8e45-781a63cd9cac', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/8ed24663-ea17-52b7-8e45-781a63cd9cac'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/14/arts/television/whats-on-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO) JACK THE GIANT SLAYER (2013) In Bryan Singer’s retooling of the “Jack and the Beanstalk” tale, a chivalrous farmhand (Nicholas Hoult) fights for a kingdom and the love of an abducted princess (Eleanor Tomlinson), reigniting an ancient war between humans and a race of giants. Bill Nighy plays General Fallon, the leader of the cannibalistic brotherhood in an otherworld called Gantua; Ewan McGregor, above, in Errol Flynn mode, plays a knight who almost becomes finger food; and Stanley Tucci is the shifty fellow to whom the royal lass has been promised. “For its first 90 minutes, ‘Jack the Giant Slayer’ is painlessly diverting,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times. “The visual design for the giants and the beanstalks keeps your eyes busy, even when the story sets your mind to wandering.” Still, she added, “in the metaphorically rich original tale, a boy wakes to discover he’s produced a mighty stalk. Here, the boy is now a man, and while the stalk is still impressive, the movie just doesn’t have staying power.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-14T06:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_des