None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korean Leader&#8217;s Aunt Appears Unscathed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jang Song-Thaek', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Il-sung', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Workers' Party of Korea", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Kyong-hui', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-15T07:07:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be8930b2-ed79-5810-a2e1-4438c3eb89a7', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be8930b2-ed79-5810-a2e1-4438c3eb89a7'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/15/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (FXX) MR. POPPER’S PENGUINS (2011) Jim Carrey — his limbs not quite as elastic in this comeback vehicle, directed by Mark Waters — manages not to be upstaged by a half-dozen flightless birds, above, as he plays Popper, a real estate wheeler-dealer and lousy father who ends up with a strange bequest from his own lousy dad. Carla Gugino is the ex-wife Popper is trying to woo back; Angela Lansbury is the reluctant seller he’s trying to persuade to part with Tavern on the Green; and David Krumholtz is the neighbor who objects to the goings-on in Popper’s snow-filled Manhattan duplex. “Mr. Carrey is the kind of performer whose shtick reliably trumps psychological sense, and the film’s blithe incoherence with respect to its main character is one of its virtues,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “It turns sentimental now and then, but it knows — Mr. Carrey knows — that what the audience wants from him is silliness.” ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-15T08:03:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4114bda-75fa-5f5d-a4e5-812865f7e85f', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4114bda-75fa-5f5d-a4e5-812865f7e85f'}, {'abstract': 'Europe does worse.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/15/if-only-it-were-the-1930s/', 'snippet': 'Europe does worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve mentioned before that at this point Europe is actually doing worse in the aftermath of the 2007 crisis than it did in the Great Depression. Nick Crafts documents this at greater length, and adds a provocative analysis of debt dynamics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If Only It Were the 1930s', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-15T13:50:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname':