ws_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/523e0d3a-0fef-535f-aa10-0d83c2840f5f', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/523e0d3a-0fef-535f-aa10-0d83c2840f5f'}, {'abstract': 'A meeting that started with discussions about the federal health care site shifted quickly to the concerns over National Security Agency spying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/18/us/politics/as-tech-industry-leaders-meet-with-obama-nsa-ruling-looms-large.html', 'snippet': 'A meeting that started with discussions about the federal health care site shifted quickly to the concerns over National Security Agency spying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama met with top technology industry executives on Tuesday to discuss two seemingly distinct controversies: a faulty health care website, and the digital surveillance practices of the National Security Agency. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/18/business/TECH/TECH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/18/business/TECH/TECH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/18/business/TECH/TECH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/18/business/TECH/TECH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1107, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Leaders and Obama Find Shared Problem: Fading Public Trust', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tech Leaders and Obama Find Shared Problem: Fading Public Trust', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calmes, Jackie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DelBene, Kurt D', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HealthCare.gov', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-17T17:04:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes and Nick Wingfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wingfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f93ce36f-1e34-5477-b8d1-8740eeebc8bf', 'word_count': 1107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f93ce36f-1e34-5477-b8d1-8740eeebc8bf'}, {'abstract': 'The only major writer to see combat in the Civil War, Bierce drew on the conflict to render his harsh judgments on American society.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/17/ambrose-bierces-civil-war/', 'snippet': 'The only major writer to see combat in the Civil War, Bierce drew on the conflict to render his harsh judgments on American society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unlike his fellow satirist, Mark Twain, who had only dabbled as a soldier for a few weeks in the summer of 1861 before deserting from his Confederate unit, Ambrose Bierce enlisted into the Union Army just days after President Lincoln issued a proclamation calling for volunteers; he went on to serve for nearly the duration of the war. He saw heavy combat in some of its bloodiest battles, including Shiloh, Chickamauga and Missionary Ridge. Although Walt Whitman and Herman Melville, among others, wrote movingly about the traumas of combat, Bierce was the only major author to have actually been a front-line soldier in the Civil War.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ambrose Bierce’s Civil War', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bierce, Ambrose', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-17T17:11:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim McWilliams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McWilliams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6bed1dc-3079-5969-bea6-20bb46c32d05', 'word_count': 2366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6bed1dc-3079-5969-bea6-20bb46c32d05'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition pays tribute to Deborah Sussman, whose work, from graphics for the Hollywood Bowl to environmental design for the 1984 Olympics, had an outsize impact on the visual culture of Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/17/on-view-the-designer-who-helped-give-l-a-its-look/', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition pays tribute to Deborah Sussman, whose work, from graphics for the Hollywood Bowl to environmental design for the 1984 Olympics, had an outsize impact on the visual culture of Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Thursday, the neon sign outside the WUHO Gallery on Hollywood Boulevard was tinted magenta for the opening of Deborah Sussman’s exhibit “Deborah Sussman Loves Los Angeles,” which collects the 82-year-old designer’s work from 1953 to 1984. The shade was chosen to honor the thread of giddy pink that has run through her entire 60-year career. Inside, an intergenerational crowd swarmed the walls lined with vivid graphics: programs for the Hollywood Bowl, brochures for LACMA, toys for Hasbro, stage design for a Rolling Stones tour and, of course, her transformative environmental design for LA 84, the 1984 Summer Olympics — all of which were inspired by the culture and landscape of her adopted city.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Designer Who Helped Give L.A. Its Look', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On View: L.A. Woman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-17T17:19:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alissa Walker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8d1f574-9a76-5c76-b1ea-22fa48415ad4', 'word_count': 633, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8d1f574-9a76-5c76-b1ea-22fa48415ad4'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama has chos