ank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-17T20:31:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10f38018-dfc4-5be0-b7c6-740016c1f8cb', 'word_count': 939, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10f38018-dfc4-5be0-b7c6-740016c1f8cb'}, {'abstract': 'Female preferences and the case for sexual restraint.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/17/sex-sociology-and-the-single-girl/', 'snippet': 'Female preferences and the case for sexual restraint.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not surprisingly, my weekend musings on the gender-specific anxieties parents might have about their daughters, and how those anxieties might possibly translate into some sort of affinity for moral traditionalism, has prompted a lot of responses from socially-liberal and feminist commentators\xa0— some huffy, some bemused, some condescending, some all three.\xa0For the purposes of this post I’m just going to work off these remarks from the New Republic’s Marc Tracy, who interviewed Adelle Waldman, the novelist whose “The Love Affairs of Nathaniel P.” served as one of my column’s touchstones, and who used their conversation as grist for the following conclusion:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sex, Sociology and the Single Girl', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Single Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'na