': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'Teton Gravity Research', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teton', 'middlename': 'Gravity', 'lastname': 'Research', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b74ccce5-a939-515b-b7cc-9c2333460d4e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b74ccce5-a939-515b-b7cc-9c2333460d4e'}, {'abstract': 'While Jeremy Jones and Bryan Iguchi readied for a descent of the Grand Teton, a spring storm hit Wyoming. As they waited for the storm to clear, the snowboarders embraced the snow.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/sports/video-snowboarders-wait-out-storm-before-grand-descent.html', 'snippet': 'While Jeremy Jones and Bryan Iguchi readied for a descent of the Grand Teton, a spring storm hit Wyoming. As they waited for the storm to clear, the snowboarders embraced the snow.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Higher Unplugged” looks at the making of the film “Higher,” which follows the snowboarder Jeremy Jones and the TGR crew as they ascend and ride peaks in Alaska, Wyoming, California and Nepal. Jones is a professional snowboarder who has been named big mountain rider of the year 10 times by Snowboarder magazine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Snowboarders Wait Out Storm Before Grand Descent', 'kicker': 'Higher Unplugged ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snowboarding', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Teton Gravity Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Jeremy (1975- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-18T17:19:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e6dcd77-f9f9-520d-80b2-76d2ac236009', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e6dcd77-f9f9-520d-80b2-76d2ac236009'}, {'abstract': 'The curious and violent life of the abolitionist Dr. Rufus Gillpatrick.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/18/doctor-teacher-soldier-spy/', 'snippet': 'The curious and violent life of the abolitionist Dr. Rufus Gillpatrick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In December 1863, a Union cavalry company escorted the disinterred remains of Dr. Rufus Gillpatrick through the streets of Lawrence, Kan., to Oread Cemetery, where they were reburied. Still marked with desolation from William Quantrill’s violent raid in August, the city remained an abolitionist stronghold and was a fitting final resting place for Dr. Gillpatrick. Although he had been, at times, a surgeon, politician, teacher, military adviser, reporter, farmer, soldier and spy, Gillpatrick was perhaps best known to contemporaries for the ardent opposition to slavery that ultimately cost him his life. His curious career and violent death illustrates the porous line between civilian and soldier on the frontier.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main':