ct', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-18T19:39:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mosi Secret', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mosi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Secret', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e49776ef-97c8-5d26-a5e2-61f9345f83d9', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e49776ef-97c8-5d26-a5e2-61f9345f83d9'}, {'abstract': 'The idiosyncratic actor discusses his return to the stage in Bruce Norris’s provocative new production at Lincoln Center.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/18/talking-talent-jeff-goldblum-star-of-the-off-broadway-play-domesticated/', 'snippet': 'The idiosyncratic actor discusses his return to the stage in Bruce Norris’s provocative new production at Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Best known for embodying diverse roles in films like “The Fly” and “The Big Chill,” Jeff Goldblum is currently making his Lincoln Center Theater debut in “Domesticated,” the Pulitzer- and Tony-winning writer Bruce Norris’s riveting exploration of gender, sexuality and love now playing at the Mitzi E. Newhouse Theater. He plays Bill Pulver, a gynecologist turned politician whose private cheating scandal becomes a media circus. With Laurie Metcalf, who plays Bill’s wife, Judy, he masterfully take the audience through the family’s public humiliation and ensuing unraveling. When “Domesticated” closes next month, Goldblum will head back to his hometown of Los Angeles to resume his weekly jazz show at the bar Rockwell, and in March he will appear in Wes Anderson’s new ensemble film, “The Grand Budapest Hotel.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/18/t-magazine/18goldblu