'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-18T21:55:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/f2b6db03-0279-544b-b595-2345644f43a3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/f2b6db03-0279-544b-b595-2345644f43a3'}, {'abstract': '“The Invisible Code,” the 10th novel in Christopher Fowler’s Peculiar Crimes Unit series, follows two Golden Age detectives as they solve a murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/books/the-invisible-code-by-christopher-fowler.html', 'snippet': '“The Invisible Code,” the 10th novel in Christopher Fowler’s Peculiar Crimes Unit series, follows two Golden Age detectives as they solve a murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My timing’s not ideal, jumping into Christopher Fowler’s Peculiar Crimes Unit series with its 10th installment, “The Invisible Code.” But better late than never. Perhaps it’s sufficient explanation of the series’s allure to say that when Mr. Fowler first conceived it, he planned to write about “a pair of intelligent Golden Age detectives who are forced to deal with the modern world.” He was advised to make them younger and to add more sex and violence, but his strengths are humor and Holmesian cogitation, and he stuck with them. He was right. One book in the series won an award for the funniest mystery novel of 2008.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/19/arts/19BOOK/19BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/19/arts/19BOOK/19BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 784}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/19/arts/19BOOK/19BO