ent_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Horch and Christopher Drew', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drew', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ccff647-2159-5216-981b-b6476bfcfc99', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ccff647-2159-5216-981b-b6476bfcfc99'}, {'abstract': 'The stylist B. Akerlund has emerged as the go-to person for pop stars who want an edgy, fashion-forward look for their videos and tours. Including looks from Beyoncé’s new album.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/fashion/B-Akerlund-Stylist-Beyonce-Visual-Album-Fashion.html', 'snippet': 'The stylist B. Akerlund has emerged as the go-to person for pop stars who want an edgy, fashion-forward look for their videos and tours. Including looks from Beyoncé’s new album.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Yesterday I woke up and it was Beyoncé, all day,” said the stylist B. Akerlund by phone on Monday from her native Sweden, where she had returned for the holidays just as the star was releasing her new self-titled album on the Internet, produced with a perhaps unprecedented canny stealth and selling a record 828,773\xa0units worldwide in less than three days. “People actually went kind of crazy about it.” ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Would Beyoncé Wear? She Knows', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Get Me The Leash!’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crane, Dan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Akerlund, B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T01:04:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Crane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b81863c3-8d79-5248-9eac-08b943689dc4', 'word_count': 2094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b81863c3-8d79-5248-9eac-08b943689dc4'}, {'abstract': 'Results from a national testing program revealed a steady but incremental improvement in student performance during the Bloomberg era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/education/new-york-city-students-show-slight-gains-on-test-scores.html', 'snippet': 'Results from a national testing program revealed a steady but incremental improvement in student performance during the Bloomberg era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City public school students posted tiny gains this year on the bulk of federal academic tests that were last administered in 2011, though the city’s fourth graders showed no growth at all in reading scores over the last two years.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York City Students Show Slight Gains on Test Scores', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Small Testing Gains for City Students Continue Gradual Rise in Bloomberg Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Assessment of Educational Progress', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T01:05:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker and Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/347434e8-0c4f-53ca-872a-19b89e0aaac2', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/347434e8-0c4f-53ca-872a-19b89e0aaac2'}, {'abstract': 'The Federal Communications Commission has asked Congress if it has the authority to repeal a 1975 rule that required sports stadiums to be sold out before broadcasting a game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/business/media/fcc-seeks-to-do-away-with-sports-blackout-rule.html', 'snippet': 'The Federal Communications Commission has asked Congress if it has the authority to repeal a 1975 rule that required sports stadiums to be sold out before broadcasting a game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Federal Communications Commission on Wednesday proposed repealing its blackout rule, which for decades has been the bane of sports fans whose ability to watch their favorite team’s home games on television has depended on whether enough of their fellow fans bought tickets to see it in person.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'F.C.C. Seeks to Do Away With Sports Blackout Rule', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'F.C.C. Seeks to Do Away With Sports Blackout Rule', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [