 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T02:13:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Macur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d519ffdc-d92c-5bb1-b0e8-fc730b330419', 'word_count': 1132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d519ffdc-d92c-5bb1-b0e8-fc730b330419'}, {'abstract': 'After inspiring many of its neighbors at the onset of the Arab Spring, it is Tunisia that again could show the way to get the transitions to democracy back on track.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/opinion/tunisias-reawakening.html', 'snippet': 'After inspiring many of its neighbors at the onset of the Arab Spring, it is Tunisia that again could show the way to get the transitions to democracy back on track.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three years ago, a 26-year-old fruit vendor named Mohamed Bouazizi set himself ablaze in the central Tunisian town of Sidi Bouzid after being shaken down and humiliated by corrupt local officials, setting off an outpouring of anger across the Arab world against tyrannical regimes. But what was hailed as the Arab Spring soon disintegrated into dismal and violent sequels, most notably in Libya, Egypt and Syria. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tunisia’s Reawakening', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tunisia’s Reawakening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010-