tml', 'snippet': 'The agreement between Kevyn D. Orr, the emergency manager, and Mike Duggan, who becomes mayor next month, establishes guidelines for managing city operations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — The city’s emergency manager on Thursday turned over major governmental responsibilities to its mayor-elect, marking a restoration of political power to the mayor as Detroit reorganizes under federal bankruptcy laws. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/us/SUB-DETROIT/SUB-DETROIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/20/us/SUB-DETROIT/SUB-DETROIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/us/SUB-DETROIT/SUB-DETROIT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/us/SUB-DETROIT/SUB-DETROIT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1361, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/us/SUB-DETROIT/SUB-DETROIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/20/us/SUB-DETROIT/SUB-DETROIT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/us/SUB-DETROIT/SUB-DETROIT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bankruptcy Manager in Detroit to Divide Duties With Mayor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bankruptcy Manager In Detroit to Divide Duties With Mayor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Orr, Kevyn D', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duggan, Mike', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T16:17:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vlasic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vlasic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c98acd76-3137-5aae-851b-0eb17dd9a3f6', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c98acd76-3137-5aae-851b-0eb17dd9a3f6'}, {'abstract': 'The Grammy Awards in January will include some unusual pairings of artists.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/daft-punk-and-kendrick-lamar-among-grammy-awards-performers/', 'snippet': 'The Grammy Awards in January will include some unusual pairings of artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some unusual pairings distinguish the first round of artists who have been announced to perform at the Grammy Awards next month. The Recording Academy said Thursday that the rapper Kendrick Lamar, who has seven Grammy nominations, including best new artist and album of the year, will perform with the rock band Imagine Dragons, nominated for record of the year and best rock performance. Pink, the pop-punk singer, will take the stage with Nate Ruess of the rock trio Fun.; both are nominated for song of the year and best pop duo/group performance for “Just Give Me a Reason.” And the helmet-headed electronic rock duo Daft Punk, an unusual pair in their own right and an infrequent television presence, will also perform on the show.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Daft Punk and Kendrick Lamar Among Grammy Awards Performers', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strange Bedfellows for the Grammy Show', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammy Awards', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T16:24:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/740c79b4-9630-56bb-8c5b-873388b1bcc1', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/740c79b4-9630-56bb-8c5b-873388b1bcc1'}, {'abstract': 'The band members drew on their rather ambiguous attitudes about home to write a tune for “August: Osage County.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/video-kings-of-leon-and-mixed-family-feelings/', 'snippet': 'The band members drew on their rather ambiguous attitudes about home to write a tune for “August: Osage County.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Along with stars like Taylor Swift, Coldplay and U2, Grammy winners Kings of Leon could become Oscar nominees soon. Their song “Last Mile Home,” featured over the credits of “August: Osage County,” is on the Academy’s list of eligible titles. At the film’s New York premiere last week, the group, brothers from Nashville, was preparing to see the final cut for the first time. They had screened an earlier version in Nashville to inspire them to write. But they didn’t include characters from Tracy Letts’s Pulitzer-prize winning dysfunctional clan in their song.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Kings of Leon and Mixed Family Feelings', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kings of Leon (Music Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T16:30:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43248748-c6ea-5bfe-a304-1838143d1bc9', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43248748-c6ea-5bfe-a304-1838143d1bc9'}, {'abstract': 'Dean Baquet, the managing editor, will review the weekly publication, with help from Deborah Needleman, the T Magazine editor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/20/business/media/times-will-study-potential-changes-to-its-sunday-magazine.html', 'snippet': 'Dean Baquet, the managing editor, will review the weekly publication, with help from Deborah Needleman, the T Magazine editor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times Magazine will be reassessed by the newspaper’s managing editor, Dean Baquet, and Deborah Needleman, the editor of T Magazine, for the next three months before a new editor is appointed, according to a memo sent Thursday to employees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Times Will Study Potential Changes to Its Sunday Magazine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T16:36:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9fc35fc-353b-5415-8dd4-4d4f9fafad75', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9fc35fc-353b-5415-8dd4-4d4f9fafad75'}, {'abstract': 'Valery Gergiev, the Russian conductor embroiled in protests over a Russian law limiting discussion of homosexuality, says the anti-discrimination law in Munich, where he is to take over the Munich Philharmonic in 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/gergiev-with-eye-on-munich-job-responds-to-antigay-accusations/', 'snippet': 'Valery Gergiev, the Russian conductor embroiled in protests over a Russian law limiting discussion of homosexuality, says the anti-discrimination law in Munich, where he is to take over the Munich Philharmonic in 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Valery Gergiev, the Russian conductor whose recent performances have been overshadowed by a debate over Russia’s law limiting the discussion of homosexuality, has assured officials in Munich that he “fully supports” the city’s stance against discrimination. Mr. Gergiev is to take over as director of the Munich Philharmonic in 2015.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gergiev, With Eye on Munich Job, Responds to Antigay Accusations', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gergiev, Valery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mariinsky Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Munich Philharmonic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Munich (Germany)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T16:53:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Eddy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eddy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e7d9071-21c4-5ffd-b870-27767195933a', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e7d9071-21c4-5ffd-b870-27767195933a'}, {'abstract': 'The research firm Muddy Waters announced an unusual proposal on Thursday: It would be willing to pay an independent auditing firm to review the results of an investigation by NQ Mobile’s independent board committee.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/12/19/muddy-waters-offers-to-pay-to-double-check-board-review-of-chinese-firm/', 'snippet': 'The research firm Muddy Waters announced an unusual proposal on Thursday: It would be willing to pay an independent auditing firm to review the results of an investigation by NQ Mobile’s independent board committee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At first, Carson C. Block accused NQ Mobile, a Chinese mobile security firm, of perpetrating a “massive fraud.” Now he’s offering to backstop an inquiry into the company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/business/dbpix-carson-block/dbpix-carson-block-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/20/business/dbpix-carson-block/dbpix-carson-block-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'c