noted. Full reviews of recent art shows: nytimes.com/art. A searchable guide to these and many other art shows is at nytimes.com/events.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Museum and Gallery Listings for Dec. 20-26', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings: Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T23:15:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/2129dec0-bb0f-508c-89c1-3e9dbf802e55', 'word_count': 7006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2129dec0-bb0f-508c-89c1-3e9dbf802e55'}, {'abstract': 'C. P. Cavafy makes no explicit reference to himself in his best and most stirring work; and yet, with every poem we read, we cannot help thinking of him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/other-countries-other-shores.html', 'snippet': 'C. P. Cavafy makes no explicit reference to himself in his best and most stirring work; and yet, with every poem we read, we cannot help thinking of him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We love poets for the things their poems lead us to imagine; but equally, we love them for how we imagine their lives to be. Confusing poets’ lives with their work is an illusion as old as the tradition of confusing words with objects. But in fact it is for the sake of this illusion that we feel such a strong need for poetry, for novels, for literature. There are some poets whose work we read with their lives in mind, and what we know of those lives ensures that their poetry leaves a more enduring impression. C. P. Cavafy is, for me, just such a poet. Like Edgar Allan Poe, like Franz Kafka, Cavafy makes no explicit reference to himself in his best and most stirring work; and yet, with every poem we read, we cannot help thinking of him.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Other Countries, Other Shores', 'kicker': 'Author’s Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Other Countries, Other Shores', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cavafy, C P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T23:16:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Orhan Pamuk', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Orhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pamuk', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67e1a13d-c1ff-51c6-8257-8afa9f1464d6', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67e1a13d-c1ff-51c6-8257-8afa9f1464d6'}, {'abstract': 'An economist argues that most humans now live longer, healthier, more connected lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/angus-deatons-great-escape.html', 'snippet': 'An economist argues that most humans now live longer, healthier, more connected lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Economic nostalgia can have a strong appeal, especially following more than five years of a financial crisis and its aftermath. In the United States, people talk longingly of the mid-20th century, when the middle class was growing and upward mobility was the norm. In Europe and Japan, many hark back to the 1980s, before the euro was born and the Japanese bubble burst. Even in China and India, two of the world’s more dynamic economies, some like to celebrate a time when life did not revolve around breakneck growth.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Leon/1222-bks-Leon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 573, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Leon/1222-bks-Leon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 573}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Leon/1222-bks-Leon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 978, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Leon/1222-bks-Leon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1957, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Leon/1222-bks-Leon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Leon/1222-bks-Leon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Leon/1222-bks-Leon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cockeyed Optimist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Cockeyed Optimist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leonhardt, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deaton, Angus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T23:16:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/adf43771-684e-5693-8550-4c835dc073ac', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/adf43771-684e-5693-8550-4c835dc073ac'}, {'abstract': 'Deborah Solomon’s biography of Rockwell, who believed in art as storytelling and created images that reside in collective memory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/american-mirror-the-life-and-art-of-norman-rockwell-by-deborah-solomon.html', 'snippet': 'Deborah Solomon’s biography of Rockwell, who believed in art as storytelling and created images that reside in collective memory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear fragile Norman Rockwell. He grew up in a series of cramped apartments and shabby boardinghouses in Upper Manhattan, skinny, weak-chinned, squinty, an abysmal student, inept at math, probably dyslexic, neglected by his parents, overshadowed by a handsome, athletic older brother, and for the rest of his life was uncertain of himself and modest to a fault. He enrolled in art school to become an illustrator in the footsteps of Howard Pyle and N. C. Wyeth, just as the Armory Show of 1913 was about to usher in the avant-garde and relegate illustration — art as storytelling — to the back pages. He was a loner, did not feel close to his parents or brother, was not a devoted father to his three sons. He was married to his work; his three wives, Irene O’Connor, Mary Barstow and Molly Punderson, learned to accommodate. His biographer reports that his love life was stark: “Nothing that can be described as a love letter survives among his papers. It seems unlikely that he ever wrote one.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22cover-sub/22cover-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22cover-sub/22cover-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 709}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22cover-sub/22cover-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 867, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22cover-sub/22cover-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1453, 'width': 1230, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22cover-sub/22cover-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22cover-sub/22cover-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22cover-sub/22cover-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Norman Rockwell, the Storyteller', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Storyteller', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rockwell, Norman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solomon, Deborah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-19T23:17:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Garrison Keillor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Garrison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keillor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/859986a2-4492-5cf0-b22c-4d157234465d', 'word_count': 1514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/859986a2-4492-5cf0-b22c-4d157234465d'}, {'abstract': 'In the family-friendly film “Walking With Dinosaurs,” computer-generated creatures face conflicts echoing the “Lion King” story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/20/movies/walking-with-dinosaurs-about-a-herd-on-the-move.html', 'snippet': 'In the family-friendly film “Walking With Dinosaurs,” computer-generated creatures face conflicts echoing the “Lion King” story.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Lion King” goes prehistoric in “Walking With Dinosaurs,” a good-looking extension of the franchise that began as a BBC television series.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/arts/20DINOSAURS/DINOSAURS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 267, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/20/arts/20DINOSAURS/DINOSAURS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 267}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/20/arts/20DINOSAURS/DINOSAURS-jumbo.jpg', 'hei