', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/sports/nflmatchups2/nflmatchups2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 16 N.F.L. Matchups', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Battles for Position With Playoffs Near', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gervino, Tony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Orleans Saints', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carolina Panthers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brees, Drew', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tony Gervino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gervino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfe79e56-9a5b-55c1-8e95-cf15ebd9b3e1', 'word_count': 1656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfe79e56-9a5b-55c1-8e95-cf15ebd9b3e1'}, {'abstract': 'My winning streak as our family’s champion pie king ended six years ago — and that’s when all the trouble started.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/20/the-pies-that-bind/', 'snippet': 'My winning streak as our family’s champion pie king ended six years ago — and that’s when all the trouble started.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My winning streak as our family’s champion pie king ended six years ago — and that’s when all the trouble started. Since going to college down South, I had sat on the holiday pie throne without fear of challenge, learning early what my Tar Heel neighbor Frances Mayes wrote in “Under the Tuscan Sun”: “[P]ecan pie [is] a necessary ingredient of Christmas.” Remaining the king was a result of the time I’d invested refining my pecan pie recipe — which, if I may — had two outstanding attributes: a generous helping of Kentucky bourbon to cut the treacly sweetness of the Karo syrup and a top secret mixing technique that prevented the pie from becoming gelatinous. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/19/science/19wellpetrow/19wellpetrow-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/19/science/19wellpetrow/19wellpetrow-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 367}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/19/science/19wellpetrow/19wellpetrow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/19/science/19wellpetrow/19wellpetrow-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1253, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/19/science/19wellpetrow/19wellpetrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/19/science/19wellpetrow/19wellpetrow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/19/science/19wellpetrow/19wellpetrow-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pies That Bind', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desserts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pecans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Petrow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petrow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fe027b0-694b-59cb-a3e2-96752c3394ad', 'word_count': 1133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fe027b0-694b-59cb-a3e2-96752c3394ad'}, {'abstract': 'There is a shadowy world of which you, innocent taxpayer, understand nothing. It’s better that way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/21/opinion/atwood-virtual-reality-real-spies.html', 'snippet': 'There is a shadowy world of which you, innocent taxpayer, understand nothing. It’s better that way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aha! It seems that national security personnel have been infiltrating the online game World of Warcraft, disguised as loathsome Orcs or brew-swilling Pandarens or pointy-eared, deep-cleavaged Blood Elves. What are these costumed undercover agents doing in the hi-def scenery, apart from having fun? The cover story is that they hope to expose hordes of terrorists laundering money in pretend banks or trading toxic info on drawbridges; or else to unearth — concealed in a surreal mushroom or luminous flower or ominous grotto — a keyhole leading to the Dark Web.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/21/opinion/21atwood/21atwood-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/21/opinion/21atwood/21atwood-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 713}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/21/opinion/21atwood/21atwood-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 862, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/21/opinion/21atwood/21atwood-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1723, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/21/opinion/21atwood/21atwood-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/21/opinion/21atwood/21atwood-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/21/opinion/21atwood/21atwood-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Virtual Reality, Real Spies', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Virtual Reality, Real Spies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T11:13:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Atwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Atwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d9feb33-8118-58f6-81ac-b36ac5fc0658', 'word_count': 1172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d9feb33-8118-58f6-81ac-b36ac5fc0658'}, {'abstract': 'The ratings agency cited less “cohesion” in the bloc in removing its AAA badge of approval, but the impact of the move was negligible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/21/business/international/sp-cuts-european-unions-credit-rating.html', 'snippet': 'The ratings agency cited less “cohesion” in the bloc in removing its AAA badge of approval, but the impact of the move was negligible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — European Union leaders gathering here for a summit meeting on Friday barely had time to congratulate themselves on their new banking deal before getting a slap in the face from the ratings agency Standard & Poor’s.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'S.&P. Cuts European Union’s Credit Rating', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'S.&P. Downgrades Credit Rating for European Union', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Ratings and Credit Rating Agencies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jolly, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Standard & Poor's Corp", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rehn, Olli', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T11:21:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88bc6cd7-f82a-5cb0-bd60-a5247f162744', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88bc6cd7-f82a-5cb0-bd60-a5247f162744'}, {'abstract': 'New Communist Party slogans draw from Confucian classics, Maoist rhetoric and Xi Jinping’s vision of “The Chinese Dream.” But many people are skeptical.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/21/opinion/sunday/murong-the-new-face-of-chinese-propaganda.html', 'snippet': 'New Communist Party slogans draw from Confucian classics, Maoist rhetoric and Xi Jinping’s vision of “The Chinese Dream.” But many people are skeptical.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — An old schoolmate of mine is now a midlevel government official. This friend, Mr. Lin, is also a member of the Chinese Communist Party. He worships Mao Zedong and often quotes Mao’s saying, “Man must have some spirit.” He once told me that when he visited New York, he most liked the Statue of Liberty, because it had “spirit,” and least liked Times Square, because it was “all about business.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/murong-xuecun/murong-xuecun-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/murong-xuecun/murong-xuecun-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/murong-xuecun/murong-xuecun-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/murong-xuecun/murong-xuecun-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/murong-xuecun/murong-xuecun-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/murong-xuecun/murong-xuecun-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/murong-xuecun/murong-xuecun-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The New Face of Chinese Propaganda', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "The New Face of China's Propaganda", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murong Xuecun', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Propaganda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T11:39:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Murong Xuecun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Murong', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Xuecun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd7593c1-f848-5b70-9a77-9109cc41df37', 'word_count': 1687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd7593c1-f848-5b70-9a77-9109cc41df37'}, {'abstract': 'A family whose home was flooded during Hurricane Sandy takes it one day at a time, and that includes buying a new Advent calendar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/nyregion/after-hurricane-sandy-reclaiming-christmas.html', 'snippet': 'A family whose home was flooded during Hurricane Sandy takes it one day at a time, and that includes buying a new Advent calendar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first day of December, from the time the children were little, I hung the Advent calendar on the door to the laundry room. I never liked that calendar much. It was ordinary, made of cloth, maybe three feet long with 25 pockets. Each day, as we counted the time left until Christmas, our four children, starting with the youngest, took turns filling another pocket with a small toy or candy cane or reindeer, and then finally, on Christmas morning, Santa’s head. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/nyregion/22ADVENT1_SPAN/22ADVENT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/nyregion/22ADVENT1_SPAN/22ADVENT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/nyregion/22ADVENT1_SPAN/22ADVENT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/nyregion/22ADVENT1_SPAN/22ADVENT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/nyregion/22ADVENT1_SPAN/22ADVENT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/nyregion/22ADVENT1_SPAN/22ADVENT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/nyregion/22ADVENT1_SPAN/22ADVENT1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Hurricane Sandy, Reclaiming Christmas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After a Storm, Reclaiming Christmas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Osborne, Wendy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Calendars', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T12:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wendy Oborne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wendy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oborne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4432e15b-bd14-5bd2-8341-46592a5b5f18', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4432e15b-bd14-5bd2-8341-46592a5b5f18'}, {'abstract': 'Businesses need to generate new ideas, but not every new idea is a good one.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/20/establishing-a-process-to-evaluate-ideas/', 'snippet': 'Businesses need to generate new ideas, but not every new idea is a good one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Innovation is one of the keys to business success. If you don’t innovate, your business will suffer. If it isn’t made obsolete by competition, it will likely end up as a commodity business with little to distinguish it from competitors. And yet, not every new idea that a business comes up with is going to be a good one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Establishing a Process to Evaluate Ideas', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parsons, Sabrina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Palo Alto Software', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T12:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Patrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patrick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/678ca3de-8997-5e10-ace7-797f2fcfd4d8', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/678ca3de-8997-5e10-ace7-797f2fcfd4d8'}, {'abstract': 'As part of efforts to curb official extravagance, the Communist Party has called for curbs on expensive funeral rites for dead officials and their relatives.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/20/china-targets-lavish-funerals-in-crackdown-on-official-corruption/', 'snippet': 'As part of efforts to curb official extravagance, the Communist Party has called for curbs on expensive funeral rites for dead officials and their relati