ine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T15:22:29+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7ffb90a9-17b8-5c24-a1cc-e523e0da888b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7ffb90a9-17b8-5c24-a1cc-e523e0da888b'}, {'abstract': 'Friends with manuscripts; strangers with embargoes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/magazine/-sub-mission-creep.html', 'snippet': 'Friends with manuscripts; strangers with embargoes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I work at a midsize publishing company. I randomly met a writer outside of work and offered to read over his unpublished manuscript. I had him send it to my work address, but it was unclear whether I was reading as a new friend or a potential publisher. I read the manuscript during work hours. It was wonderful. I’m certain he would be able to get picked up by a competing company — one with better publicity and marketing resources than my own, more willing to give him higher compensation and greater attention. This could be a chance for our company to publish something special, and I would receive credit for acquiring a new author. But as a friend, I would never recommend us. To whom am I ethically obligated here, this writer or my company? NAME WITHHELD, NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': Non