y The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d204128-494f-5ec7-92ff-23a8d029011a', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d204128-494f-5ec7-92ff-23a8d029011a'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMERICA’S GREAT GAME: The CIA’s Secret Arabists and the Shaping of the Modern Middle East,  by Hugh Wilford. (Basic Books, $29.99.) How the C.I.A., pushed by John Foster Dulles, lost the respect of the Arab world in the 1950s.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:12:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a97c0da-3168-58c3-9826-f1049ef2f981', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a97c0da-3168-58c3-9826-f1049ef2f981'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHOOTING VICTORIA: Madness, Mayhem, and the Rebirth of the British Monarchy,  by Paul Thomas Murphy. (Pegasus, $18.95.) Between 1840 and 1882, while thrones across Europe toppled, there were eight unsuccessful attempts on Queen Victoria’s life. Murphy’s book — a learned ramble through history, literature, penology, constitutional theory and even ballistics — argues that the would-be assassins cemented Victoria’s popularity and helped preserve the British monarchy.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:18:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b089d6b8-58f1-5104-b4c0-28f046a217f0', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b089d6b8-58f1-5104-b4c0-28f046a217f0'}, {'abstract': 'In Katherine Rundell’s middle-grade novel, an orphan befriends children who live on top of Parisian buildings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/rooftoppers-by-katherine-rundell.html', 'snippet': 'In Katherine Rundell’s middle-grade novel, an orphan befriends children who live on top of Parisian buildings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In many classic children’s novels, parents are a problem best dispensed with early on. Life only gets interesting — if, typically, more unpleasant — once they are out of the way. Hence the popularity of the orphan plot, from Dickens to Rowling, and, lately, its contemporary cousin, the single-parent plot (see “The Hunger Games” and “Twilight”).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Survival Skills', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Survival Skills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Eakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e730c687-7378-5d46-aba5-2d11e1cfe21b', 'word_count': 715, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e730c687-7378-5d46-aba5-2d11e1cfe21b'}, {'abstract': 'A boy is overwhelmed by rage when his deeply unhappy family appears on a TV show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/reality-boy-by-a-s-king.html', 'snippet': 'A boy is overwhelmed by rage when his deeply unhappy family appears on a TV show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a 2009 episode of the television show “Wife Swap,” a pudgy 7-year-old named Curtis was paired for a while with a mom from another family who emphasized good eating habits, waking up early and other affronts to 7-year-olds everywhere. Curtis’s on-air retorts, including “Bacon is good for me” and “Chicken nuggets is like my family,” made him a meme online, where he is still known as King Curtis.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lights, Camera, Dysfunction', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lights, Camera, Dysfunction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:20:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ade9b0ec-1ebd-5270-8f32-4d1c93365f2a', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ade9b0ec-1ebd-5270-8f32-4d1c93365f2a'}, {'abstract': 'Women’s bodies are the battlegrounds in Susann Cokal’s novel of palace intrigue set during the Scandinavian Renaissance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/susann-cokals-kingdom-of-little-wounds.html', 'snippet': 'Women’s bodies are the battlegrounds in Susann Cokal’s novel of palace intrigue set during the Scandinavian Renaissance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like a fairy tale, “The Kingdom of Little Wounds” starts with a pin’s prick and a drop of blood. Ava Bingen, a seamstress and the book’s main character, must quickly mend Queen Isabel’s gown, which has ripped in the midst of a ball. Her needle slips and she pricks royal flesh. It’s 1572 in the Scandinavian city of Skyggehavn, and Ava, for her incompetence, is thrown into the dungeon.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Precious Than Rubies', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Precious Than Rubies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cokal, Susann', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:20:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Darcey Steinke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Darcey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/25f02d3b-2808-5337-8f5c-0894277951f3', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25f02d3b-2808-5337-8f5c-0894277951f3'}, {'abstract': 'Thoughts of snowstorms — real or imagined — preoccupy the children in three picture books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/jonathan-beans-big-snow-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Thoughts of snowstorms — real or imagined — preoccupy the children in three picture books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My 3-year old daughter knows nothing of snow. She’d only just turned 2 when we moved to Rome — a spellbinding city in many ways but one where snow rarely falls. (In 2012, a blizzard ground the city to a halt, and Romans are still talking about it with wide-eyed amazement.) My daughter can’t possibly remember the winters of her New York babyhood, but suddenly, about six months ago, she began to ask me when the snow would come. When would we play in the snow? Where was the snow hiding? I could only guess she was exhibiting a child’s sixth sense for wonder: Snow is a portal out of the ordinary churn of life, forcing even grown-ups to go out and play. Coincidentally, and luckily for my daughter, three new picture books herald the majesty of snow, bringing its almost magically transformative power to any child who yearns for it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22casey-span/22casey-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22casey-span/22casey-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22casey-span/22casey-span-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 593, 'width': 953, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22casey-span/22casey-span-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 593, 'width': 953, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22casey-span/22casey-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22casey-span/22casey-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22casey-span/22casey-span-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Winter Wonders', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Winter Wonders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Nell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bean, Jonathan C (1979- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:21:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nell Casey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2baa8812-5699-586e-a4fe-d979add90a12', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2baa8812-5699-586e-a4fe-d979add90a12'}, {'abstract': 'Josef Joffe disputes the popular perception that America’s supremacy is on the wane.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/the-myth-of-americas-decline-by-josef-joffe.html', 'snippet': 'Josef Joffe disputes the popular perception that America’s supremacy is on the wane.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vice president Joe Biden was wrong when he said, at last year’s Democratic Convention, “It’s never been a good bet to bet against the American people.” In fact, claiming that America is going to hell in a handbasket has been among the more reliable routes to both the Oval Office and the best-seller lists. Josef Joffe, the publisher-editor of the German weekly Die Zeit, points out in his new book, “The Myth of America’s Decline,” that the United States has been dogged for much of its history as a superpower by warnings of doom.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Caldwell/1222-bks-Caldwell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 650, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Caldwell/1222-bks-Caldwell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 650}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Caldwell/1222-bks-Caldwell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 946, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Caldwell/1222-bks-Caldwell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1531, 'width': 1414, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Caldwell/1222-bks-Caldwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Caldwell/1222-bks-Caldwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Caldwell/1222-bks-Caldwell-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reigning Heavyweight', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reigning Heavyweight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joffe, Josef', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:21:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Caldwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caldwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d36a4cb7-e188-5aae-affe-f624ad3380db', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d36a4cb7-e188-5aae-affe-f624ad3380db'}, {'abstract': 'From lost phrases to newly coined terms, three works present a range of definitions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/the-book-of-jezebel-the-horologicon-and-wordbirds.html', 'snippet': 'From lost phrases to newly coined terms, three works present a range of definitions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve ever doubted that individual words can be a window into a whole culture, “The Book of Jezebel,” “The Horologicon” and “Wordbirds” will change your mind.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 831}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 740, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1479, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub/1222-bks-Fogarty-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Other Words', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Other Words', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Anna', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hess, Amanda', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forsyth, Mark', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zechel, Elizabeth', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:23:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mignon Fogarty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mignon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fogarty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/75b70312-00cf-5f0e-a1f0-ca637ce3de38', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75b70312-00cf-5f0e-a1f0-ca637ce3de38'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Auster’s memoir traces the history of his psychological development from childhood to early adulthood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/paul-austers-report-from-the-interior.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Auster’s memoir traces the history of his psychological development from childhood to early adulthood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Writing multiple novels is generally considered a triumph, writing multiple memoirs a somewhat shameful habit. Still, I’ve never heard anyone sniff about Paul Auster’s autobiographical recidivism; perhaps his 16 acclaimed novels compensate for it. In any case, on the basis of his five memoirs alone, Auster should be recognized as one of the great American prose stylists of our time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22manguso-sub/22manguso-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22manguso-sub/22manguso-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22manguso-sub/22manguso-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22manguso-sub/22manguso-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22manguso-sub/22manguso-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22manguso-sub/22manguso-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22manguso-sub/22manguso-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Correspondence to Self', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Correspondence to Self', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manguso, Sarah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Auster, Paul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:23:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Manguso', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manguso', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/51061507-4fa4-5832-bfa9-9ab53823c1a9', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51061507-4fa4-5832-bfa9-9ab53823c1a9'}, {'abstract': 'The lives of Anne Marie Wirth Cauchon’s young narrators intersect in a Montana town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/nothing-by-anne-marie-wirth-cauchon.html', 'snippet': 'The lives of Anne Marie Wirth Cauchon’s young narrators intersect in a Montana town.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The strain of holding the I together adheres to the I in all stages; and the temptation to lose it has always been there with the blind determination to maintain it.” This quotation, from Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer’s “Dialectic of Enlightenment,” is the first of the three epigraphs of “Nothing,” Anne Marie Wirth Cauchon’s bluntly apocalyptic and psychologically attentive first novel. Her two I’s, Ruth and James, who take turns narrating the book’s brisk chapters (until later, when Ruth takes over most narrating duties), are young drifters who seem to be in their early 20s and have separately left Minneapolis for the valley town of Missoula, Mont.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Continental Drifters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Continental Drifters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Nothing (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wirth Cauchon, Anne Marie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:23:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tao Lin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc40b37a-0b2a-529d-b387-c968ef628ac7', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc40b37a-0b2a-529d-b387-c968ef628ac7'}, {'abstract': 'The most notorious operative of Pinkerton’s detective agency, James McParland was a thorn in the sides of unions and outlaws.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/pinkertons-great-detective-by-beau-riffenburgh.html', 'snippet': 'The most notorious operative of Pinkerton’s detective agency, James McParland was a thorn in the sides of unions and outlaws.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lobby of the Idanha Hotel in Boise, Idaho, was the scene of great excitement in the spring and summer of 1907. There, most mornings, sat a large man with a luxurious, curled mustache, smoking cigars and loudly holding forth, surrounded by newspaper reporters and gawping onlookers. Behind him, guarding against the threat of assassination, stood a former cowboy, armed with a Colt .45 and a 20-inch blade hidden inside a walking stick. “Most pass by with awed looks,” a witness wrote, “while some few are proud to be seen sitting alongside and basking in the great man’s halo.” One of those who stopped to stare was the actress Ethel Barrymore, in town with a touring company. The object of curiosity introduced himself: “I’m a Pinkerton man.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22MACINTYRE/22MACINTYRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 556, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22MACINTYRE/22MACINTYRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 556}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22MACINTYRE/22MACINTYRE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 948, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22MACINTYRE/22MACINTYRE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1413, 'width': 1526, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22MACINTYRE/22MACINTYRE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22MACINTYRE/22MACINTYRE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22MACINTYRE/22MACINTYRE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Law Unto Himself', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Law Unto Himself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riffenburgh, Beau', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McParland, James', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:24:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Macintyre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macintyre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbcd3f4b-dffe-5adc-a3f6-d9da5e8b6934', 'word_count': 1259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbcd3f4b-dffe-5adc-a3f6-d9da5e8b6934'}, {'abstract': 'The narrators of linked novellas relate tales of loss and the search for spiritual solace.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/bruce-wagners-empty-chair.html', 'snippet': 'The narrators of linked novellas relate tales of loss and the search for spiritual solace.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Certain writers are driven by a distilled, coherent rage. This rage may have started as a red-hot boil, but the best of these writers don’t approach the page until the original insult has cooled and the words crackle like ice. Personally, I find these folks terrifying. I like to read them, but I’m not so sure I want to know them. Their humor is barbed, their policy, scorched earth. So let’s just say that if I were to catch a glimpse of Bruce Wagner — the grumpy, gimlet-eyed, savagely funny chronicler of all things West Coast — across a crowded room, I’d likely slink out the door. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Shapiro/1222-bks-Shapiro-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Shapiro/1222-bks-Shapiro-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 447}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Shapiro/1222-bks-Shapiro-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Shapiro/1222-bks-Shapiro-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1340, 'width': 1799, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Shapiro/1222-bks-Shapiro-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Shapiro/1222-bks-Shapiro-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/1222-bks-Shapiro/1222-bks-Shapiro-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Transcendent States', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Transcendent States', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Bruce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T16:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dani Shapiro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shapiro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b21a6bb-75a3-5cbd-a6b6-69eb6b007e77', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b21a6bb-75a3-5cbd-a6b6-69eb6b007e77'}, {'abstract': 'Returned from Iraq, a young man searches for a new life in Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/books/review/the-apartment-by-greg-baxter.html', 'snippet': 'Returned from Iraq, a young man searches for a new life in Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is the distance between here and there, between now and then, between right and wrong? In Greg Baxter’s pellucid first novel, “The Apartment,” it may be simply the length of a day — but a day in which one travels surprisingly far, literally and figuratively. Baxter, whose only previous book is a work of nonfiction, “A Preparation for Death,” which details his implosion and rebirth as a writer and a person, has written a novel of subtle beauty and quiet grace; I found myself hanging on every simple word, as tense about the consequences of a man finding an apartment as if I were reading about a man defusing a bomb. And, possibly, that is what “The Apartment” is as well: the analysis of an explosion deferred, or narrowly avoided, a hairbreadth escape. It is one of the best novels I have read in a long time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22DERASMO/22DERASMO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22DERASMO/22DERASMO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 821}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22DERASMO/22DERASMO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 749, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22DERASMO/22DERASMO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2027, 'width': 1482, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/books/review/22DERASMO/22DERASMO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_