ent_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel Mehr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mehr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/00a685ba-09e5-5fdf-a354-f837ea1a77cf', 'word_count': 718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00a685ba-09e5-5fdf-a354-f837ea1a77cf'}, {'abstract': 'During Christmas week, Carnegie Hall is surprisingly active, starting on Sunday afternoon at 3, when the Met Orchestra performs an all-Mahler program conducted by James Levine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/arts/music/levine-and-mahler-laredo-and-mozart.html', 'snippet': 'During Christmas week, Carnegie Hall is surprisingly active, starting on Sunday afternoon at 3, when the Met Orchestra performs an all-Mahler program conducted by James Levine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During Christmas week, Carnegie Hall is surprisingly active, starting on Sunday afternoon at 3, when the Met Orchestra performs an all-Mahler program conducted by James Levine. The compelling baritone Peter Mattei will be the soloist in “Song of a Wayfarer.” Then Mr. Levine will lead the teeming, episodic yet inexorable Seventh Symphony, an 80-minute workout for a maestro and orchestra. Then the conductor Jaime Laredo presents two programs with the New York String Orchestra, a group of select young musicians in training. On Tuesday, Christmas Eve, at 7 p.m., he leads Mozart’s Overture to “Le Nozze di Figaro” and “Paris” Symphony, as well as Mendelssohn’s Violin Concerto with Bella Hristova as soloist. On Saturday at 8 p.m., the esteemed pianist Leon Fleisher will be heard in Prokofiev’s Concerto No. 4 for left hand and orchestra, on a program that opens with Elgar’s “Introduction and Allegro” and ends with Tchaikovsky’s “Pathétique” Symphony. (212-247-7800, carnegiehall.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22Guide3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22Guide3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22Guide3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22Guide3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22Guide3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22Guide3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22Guide3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Levine and Mahler, Laredo and Mozart', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Classical Music', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Levine and Mahler, Laredo and Mozart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T21:02:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9c6297b-5d58-596d-92a4-abf49e51a488', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9c6297b-5d58-596d-92a4-abf49e51a488'}, {'abstract': 'The indie rapper Le1f (born Khalif Diouf) is an oddball and an empire builder: an openly gay rapper with a degree in dance who also runs a label, Camp & Street.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/arts/music/an-indie-rapper-loud-and-proud.html', 'snippet': 'The indie rapper Le1f (born Khalif Diouf) is an oddball and an empire builder: an openly gay rapper with a degree in dance who also runs a label, Camp & Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The indie rapper Le1f (born Khalif Diouf) is an oddball and an empire builder: an openly gay (or as one of his songs puts it, “gayngsta”) rapper with a degree in dance who also runs a label, Camp & Street. He produced the Das Racist track “Combination Pizza Bell and Taco Hut” before starting his own busy, prolific career, including two generous free mixtapes. He has a low voice, a startling wardrobe and an ear for sparse but insistent tracks.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Indie Rapper, Loud and Proud', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Indie Rapper, Loud and Proud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diouf, Khalif (Le1f) (1989- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T21:03:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf91b989-4471-5bd4-96e1-95b14f166b88', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf91b989-4471-5bd4-96e1-95b14f166b88'}, {'abstract': 'A dancegoer’s options are fairly limited this week: There’s “The Nutcracker,” and then there’s “The Nutcracker.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/arts/dance/the-land-of-sweets-sheds-its-tutus.html', 'snippet': 'A dancegoer’s options are fairly limited this week: There’s “The Nutcracker,” and then there’s “The Nutcracker.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A dancegoer’s options are fairly limited this week: There’s “The Nutcracker,” and then there’s “The Nutcracker.” But two of the season’s most popular renditions couldn’t be more different. At Lincoln Center, New York City Ballet soldiers on in its six-week run of George Balanchine’s classic version, with its pristine dancing and reverence for childhood fantasy. A darker sort of fantasy is playing out at the Minetta Lane Theater, home to Company XIV’s burlesque twist on this E. T. A. Hoffmann tale. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22SiloGuide2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22SiloGuide2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 685}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22SiloGuide2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 897, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22SiloGuide2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1794, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22SiloGuide2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22SiloGuide2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE2/22SiloGuide2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Land of Sweets Sheds Its Tutus', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Land of Sweets Sheds Its Tutus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T21:05:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8eb18bad-519f-5ae1-83a5-c001bca1f9a0', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8eb18bad-519f-5ae1-83a5-c001bca1f9a0'}, {'abstract': 'The owner of one of the largest taxi fleets in New York has agreed to pay over $1.2 million in fines and back pay to drivers.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/20/cash-for-cab-drivers/', 'snippet': 'The owner of one of the largest taxi fleets in New York has agreed to pay over $1.2 million in fines and back pay to drivers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York’s incoming mayor, Bill de Blasio has enjoyed strong support from taxicab fleet owners, while the current Taxi Commissioner, David Yassky, has been a strong advocate for taxicab drivers. Perhaps not surprisingly, Mr. de Blasio has made it clear that Mr. Yassky, will be one of the first people out the door come\xa0January 1.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cash for Cab Drivers', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freidman, Evgeny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneiderman, Eric T', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yassky, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T21:06:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eleanor Randolph', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eleanor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Randolph', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7971fb27-81cc-5968-9616-3d9d93dc3625', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7971fb27-81cc-5968-9616-3d9d93dc3625'}, {'abstract': '“The Sing-Off,” never became a ratings juggernaut and was apparently dead at the end of its third season in 2011. Then the network had a change of heart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/arts/television/a-cappella-contest-makes-a-comeback.html', 'snippet': '“The Sing-Off,” never became a ratings juggernaut and was apparently dead at the end of its third season in 2011. Then the network had a change of heart.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Voice” has paid a lot of bills for NBC, but another good singing competition, “The Sing-Off,” never became a ratings juggernaut and was apparently dead at the end of its third season in 2011. Then the network had a change of heart. It decided to bring “The Sing-Off” back this month for a two-week, seven-episode Season 4 and gave its premiere “The Voice” as a lead-in. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE6_SPAN/22Guide6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE6_SPAN/22Guide6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE6_SPAN/22Guide6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE6_SPAN/22Guide6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE6_SPAN/22Guide6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE6_SPAN/22Guide6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE6_SPAN/22Guide6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cappella Contest Makes a Comeback', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Cappella Contest Makes a Comeback', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-20T21:07:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ce0f379-ca55-5b76-b619-7d354056e4db', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ce0f379-ca55-5b76-b619-7d354056e4db'}, {'abstract': 'Trace the history of the finest pieces of postwar art, and many of them lead back to Ileana Sonnabend, the gallery owner and collector, who died in 2007.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/arts/design/star-of-the-show-the-art-dealer.html', 'snippet': 'Trace the history of the finest pieces of postwar art, and many of them lead back to Ileana Sonnabend, the gallery owner and collector, who died in 2007.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trace the history of the finest pieces of postwar art, and many of them lead back to Ileana Sonnabend, the gallery owner and collector, who died in 2007. Andy Warhol’s “Liz #1 (Early Colored Liz),” which sold at Sotheby’s for $18 million last month? Sonnabend. Robert Rauschenberg’s 20th-century mixed-media collage “Canyon,” which the Museum of Modern Art won over the Metropolitan Museum last year? Sonnabend. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE1/22Guide1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 298, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/arts/22GUIDE1/22Guide1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600