nization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/004dbf2d-0c47-5ac7-9fad-e402a9ea6c40', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/004dbf2d-0c47-5ac7-9fad-e402a9ea6c40'}, {'abstract': 'Ruslana, a Ukrainian pop singer, has been an inspiration and a force for calm. Just don’t call her a leader of the popular movement against the government.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/20/eurovision-star-becomes-voice-of-protest/', 'snippet': 'Ruslana, a Ukrainian pop singer, has been an inspiration and a force for calm. Just don’t call her a leader of the popular movement against the government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Ukrainian security forces stormed Kiev’s central square on Dec. 11, the pop singer Ruslana was on stage and attempting to defuse the situation. “I kept a quiet confidence in myself, and I believe that my voice kept them calm,” she said recently. Ruslana, the 2004 winner of the EuroVision Song Contest, has been a constant presence at Independence Square, also known as Maidan, since demonstrators gathered there in late November to protest the government’s decision to postpone an economic agreement with the European Union. She is on stage every night from midnight until sunrise, keeping thousands of demonstrators upbeat and energized in freezing temperatures. Many protesters consider her to be one of the leaders of the movement, but she vehemently disagrees. “We said that one cannot lead Maidan, one can only join Maidan,” she said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'EuroVision Star Becomes Voice of Protest', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eurovision Song Contest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-21T04:52:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Orr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f77d215b-e8e0-5ba7-b142-141d7968cf98', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f77d215b-e8e0-5ba7-b142-141d7968cf98'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Clark’s successful child molestation lawsuit against the Boy Scouts of America in Oregon led to the release of a trove of documents containing thousands of accusations of sexual abuse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/21/us/kelly-clark-a-lawyer-who-won-a-pivotal-abuse-case-against-the-boy-scouts-dies-at-56.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Clark’s successful child molestation lawsuit against the Boy Scouts of America in Oregon led to the release of a trove of documents containing thousands of accusations of sexual abuse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kelly Clark, a lawyer whose successful child molestation lawsuit against the Boy Scouts of America in Oregon led to the release of a trove of documents containing thousands of accusations of sexual abuse, died on Tuesday at the Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minn. He was 56. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/21/obituaries/21Clark/21Clark-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/21/obituaries/21Clark/21Clark-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 773}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/2