, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/451dcdb3-207f-5a9a-a973-30b33c7c4c55', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/451dcdb3-207f-5a9a-a973-30b33c7c4c55'}, {'abstract': 'How to give away a revolutionary idea? For one college student in the late 1990s, it would take a lot of work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/22/jobs/the-not-a-scientists-assistant.html', 'snippet': 'How to give away a revolutionary idea? For one college student in the late 1990s, it would take a lot of work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was 20 years old, going to college in New York and in need of a job. My roommate waitressed at a restaurant where she made huge tips, but I didn’t have the bone structure or hair texture to be hired somewhere like that. Instead, I waited outside the Village Voice offices. It was the late ’90s, and every Wednesday when the paper came out, a line of people stretched down the sidewalk to get a first look at the classified ads. That’s how different things were before the Internet. Back then, people would unbend their legs and assume a vertical position, known as standing. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/business/22-ONWORK/22-ONWORK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/22/business/22-ONWORK/22-ONWORK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/business/22-ONWORK/22-ONWORK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/22/busine