-856c-2d4a5c8d191b', 'word_count': 1380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b98489a-756f-5f83-856c-2d4a5c8d191b'}, {'abstract': 'Here is a citizen’s guide to the great debate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/23/opinion/inequality-for-dummies.html', 'snippet': 'Here is a citizen’s guide to the great debate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inequality is in. The president, you have probably heard, has declared income inequality to be “the defining challenge of our time.” (Except he didn’t quite, but we’ll get to that.) Politicians, pundits and activists on the left have seized on the president’s words, along with the rising fortunes of progressive idols Elizabeth Warren and Bill de Blasio, to refute the apostles of austerity (mostly Republicans these days) and lay down early populist markers for the 2016 elections. Liberals of a more centrist bent — notably the former Clintonites at the Third Way think tank — have refused to join the chorus and been lashed by fellow Democrats for their blasphemy. Senator Warren has suggested that liberals who disagree with her are in the pocket of Wall Street. Third Way executives took to the op-ed page of The Wall Street Journal to accuse the populists of indulging a “ ‘we can have it all’ fantasy.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/09/18/opinion/Keller_New/Keller_New-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/09/18/opinion/Keller_New/Keller_New-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/09/18/opinion/Keller_New/Keller_New-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 350, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/09/18/opinion/Keller_New/Keller_New-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/09/18/opinion/Keller_New/Keller_New-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/09/18/opinion/Keller_New/Keller_New-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/09/18/opinion/Keller_New/Keller_New-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inequality for Dummies', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inequality for Dummies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-23T02:54:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2954173a-e486-5bf8-8718-3eedede65dbe', 'word_count': 1659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2954173a-e486-5bf8-8718-3eedede65dbe'}, {'abstract': 'The new company being created to publish The Chicago Tribune and other papers will have to pay rent and a dividend to its former parent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/23/business/media/plan-for-tribune-spinoff-raises-concerns-for-future-of-newspaper-operations.html', 'snippet': 'The new company 