': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b308ee52-689c-59f2-8a83-a187168fc80d', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b308ee52-689c-59f2-8a83-a187168fc80d'}, {'abstract': 'This winter dessert is adapted from a recipe from the French chef Olympe Versini’s cookbook, “Olympe.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/24/health/grapefruit-and-navel-orange-gratin.html', 'snippet': 'This winter dessert is adapted from a recipe from the French chef Olympe Versini’s cookbook, “Olympe.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This winter dessert is adapted from a recipe from the French chef Olympe Versini’s cookbook, “Olympe.” The creamy custard that naps the fruit is made with orange and grapefruit juice instead of milk (with a small amount of crème fraîche thrown in). Since oranges vary in size, I’m giving you a weight rather than a number. You may not use the whole amount but I’m sure you’ll find a way to enjoy any leftover oranges. I used about eight small Valencia oranges in all for this but they were about a third as heavy – though juicer — as big navel oranges.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24recipehealth/24recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 671, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'supe