 as a bystander, but chest compressions alone could save a loved one who has fallen into sudden cardiac arrest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Millions of people have been trained in CPR in recent decades, yet when people who aren’t in hospitals collapse from a sudden cardiac arrest, relatively few bystanders attempt resuscitation. Only one-fourth to one-third of those who might be helped by CPR receive it before paramedics arrive.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24BROD/24BROD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24BROD/24BROD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 630}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24BROD/24BROD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 976, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24BROD/24BROD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1661, 'width': 1583, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24BROD/24BROD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24BROD/24BROD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/24BROD/24BROD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How CPR Can Save a Life', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Personal Health: Taking Life Into Your Own Hands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'First Aid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-23T20:57:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane E. Brody', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Brody', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/547a5283-8551-5b45-9db4-8062f8e5f69b', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/547a5283-8551-5b45-9db4-8062f8e5f69b'}, {'abstract': 'If a choir committed both to socialism and a rule that limits membership to the children of Communist Party officials seems like a contradiction, perhaps it is because of the increasingly malleable notion of what it means to be “red” in China today.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/23/choir-with-elite-pedigree-promotes-red-songs-and-red-values/', 'snippet': 'If a choir committed both to socialism and a rule that limits membership to the children of Communist Party officials seems like a contradiction, perhaps it is because of the increasingly malleable notion of what it means to be “red” in China toda...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beneath the shimmering lights of a bar in western Beijing, a group of roughly two dozen retirees fell still under the baton of the 64-year-old choir conductor Li Xiaojin. With a flick of his wrist, Mr. Li sent the Beijing Founding Figures Group into a sonorous rendition of “Longing for the Red Army,” a well-known Communist revolutionary number:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',