continue to show clearly that federal budget deficits stem largely from the policies of George W. Bush rather than any initiatives by Barack Obama, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett held senior policy roles in the Reagan and George H.W. Bush administrations and served on the staffs of Representatives Jack Kemp and Ron Paul. He is the author of “The Benefit and the Burden: Tax Reform — Why We Need It and What It Will Take.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Budget Deficit as Seen From 2009', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolfers, Justin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brookings Institution', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'White House Council of Economic Advisers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-24T05:01:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/816edcbf-bfbc-5176-b0d2-fb04c62f863e', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/816edcbf-bfbc-5176-b0d2-fb04c62f863e'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Turing, a British mathematician, was convicted on charges of homosexuality in 1952; he committed suicide two years later at the age of 41.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/24/world/europe/alan-turing-enigma-code-breaker-and-computer-pioneer-wins-royal-pardon.html', 'snippet': 'Alan Turing, a British mathematician, was convicted on charges of homosexuality in 1952; he committed suicide two years later at the age of 41.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly 60 years after his death, Alan Turing, the British mathematician regarded as one of the central figures in the development of the computer, received a formal pardon from Queen Elizabeth II on Monday for his conviction in 1952 on charges of homosexuality, at the time a criminal offense in Britain. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/world/24turing1/24turing1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/24/world/24turing1/24turing1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 813}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/world/24turing1/24turing1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 756, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/world/24turing1/24turing1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1511, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/world/24turing1/24turing1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/24/world/24turing1/24turing1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None