es in southeastern Brazil, have left at least 30 people dead and forced about 50,000 people from their homes, civil defense authorities said Tuesday. Dozens of cities in both states declared states of emergency after some victims died in landslides and flash floods. President Dilma Rousseff flew to Vitória, the capital of Espírito Santo, where she announced that more than $260 million in public funds would be channeled to disaster relief efforts. Brazil’s army was also being mobilized to repair bridges in affected areas, officials said. Meteorologists in Espírito Santo said that parts of the state were struggling with the heaviest rains in 90 years. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/25/world/BRAZIL-FLOATER/BRAZIL-FLOATER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/25/world/BRAZIL-FLOATER/BRAZIL-FLOATER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/25/world/BRAZIL-FLOATER/BRAZIL-FLOATER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/25/world/BRAZIL-FLOATER/BRAZIL-FLOATER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/25/world/BRAZIL-FLOATER/BRAZIL-FLOATER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/25/world/BRAZIL-FLOATER/BRAZIL-FLOATER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth