', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f09fe10-b35b-54ea-b3bf-3e5ce264c6a7', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f09fe10-b35b-54ea-b3bf-3e5ce264c6a7'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/25/classical-playlist-thomas-ads-benjamin-hochman-angela-hewitt-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': '‘LIEUX RETROUVÉS’Steven Isserlis, cellist; Thomas Adès, pianist; works by Liszt, Janacek, Fauré, Gyorgy Kurtag, Mr. Adès.(Hyperion)The British composer Thomas Adès wrote “Lieux retrouvés” (“Revisited Places”), a moody, ingenious and fiendishly difficult 18-minute cello and piano duo, specifically for his colleague and countryman Steven Isserlis, the brilliant cellist. Mr. Adès, a formidable pianist, plays the pieces with Mr. Isserlis on an exciting recording along with works by Liszt, Janacek, Fauré and Gyorgy Kurtag — an inspired grouping of diverse composers that highlights surprising musical connections between their styles. (Anthony Tommasini)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/arts/classical-ades/classical-ades-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/24/arts/classical-ades/classical-ades-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/