ed around South Sudan’s oil resources seems the best chance for outsiders to prevent civil war, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson, former chief economist of the International Monetary Fund, is the Ronald A. Kurtz Professor of Entrepreneurship at the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management and co-author of “White House Burning: The Founding Fathers, Our National Debt, and Why It Matters to You.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Preventing Civil War in South Sudan', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ban Ki-moon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Power, Samantha', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Global Witness', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Sudan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-26T05:01:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04ddc7e8-0c41-5910-8147-7b37e83fe48e', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04ddc7e8-0c41-5910-8147-7b37e83fe48e'}, {'abstract': 'Elizabeth Kenny’s emphasis on the care of individual polio patients and close bedside observation could not be more relevant in today’s era dominated by randomized controlled trials.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/26/a-nurse-gains-fame-in-the-days-of-polio/', 'snippet': 'Elizabeth Kenny’s emphasis on the care of individual polio patients and close bedside observation could not be more relevant in today’s era dominated by randomized controlled trials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the years after World War II, polls perennially showed that Eleanor Roosevelt was the woman whom Americans admired most in the world. But in a 1951 Gallup poll, that distinction went to an Australian nurse, Elizabeth Kenny, popularly known as Sister Kenny. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/17/health/17well_polio/17well_polio-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 457, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/17/health/17well_polio/17well_polio-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 457}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/17/health/17well_polio/17well_polio-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 779, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/17/health/17well_polio/17well_polio-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1559, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/17/health/17well_polio/17well_polio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/17/health/17well_polio/17well_polio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/17/health/17well_polio/17well_polio-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Nurse Gains Fame in the Days of Polio', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Forgotten Warrior Against Polio', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physical Therapy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poliomyelitis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenny, Elizabeth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-26T05:01:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barron H. Lerner, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barron', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Lerner', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4215caeb-86ea-5fa8-9035-7ce84bfebb4a', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4215caeb-86ea-5fa8-9035-7ce84bfebb4a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/26/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:35 A.M. (Cinemax) SHAUN OF THE DEAD (2004) In this horror comedy directed by Edgar Wright, Simon Pegg plays Shaun, a worthless layabout who seems content to go nowhere fast just as long as he can hang out at his favorite pub with his best friend, Ed (Nick Frost), and his girlfriend, Liz (Kate Ashfield). But when Liz dumps Shaun, he decides to get his life together to win her back. Of course there’s also the small matter of a zombie apocalypse going on outside his door. Writing in The New York Times, Stephen Holden said that the film skillfully satirized the zombie genre by “implying that Britain may already be populated with the living dead, metaphorically speaking.” He added, “The motley Londoners who face down the creatures are so inept and unthinking in their response to the catastrophe that the uncharitable part of you may decide that they deserve their probable (but not guaranteed) fates during what panicked television commentators label Z-Day.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-26T06:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/016405f3-2f29-580c-a374-947a43777ee4', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/016405f3-2f29-580c-a374-947a43777ee4'}, {'abstract': 'A visit to the Yasukuni Shrine highlighted Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s willingness to take on big political risks as he tries to build a more powerful Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/27/world/asia/japanese-premier-visits-contentious-war-shrine.html', 'snippet': 'A visit to the Yasukuni Shrine highlighted Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s willingness to take on big political risks as he tries to build a more powerful Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Shinzo Abe’s past year as prime minister has concentrated chiefly on reviving Japan’s long-ailing economy. Yet in Mr. Abe’s mind, the country’s newfound economic prowess is a means to an end: to build a more powerful, assertive Japan, complete with a full-fledged military, as well as pride in its World War II-era past.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/world/japan/japan-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/27/world/japan/japan-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/world/japan/japan-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/world/japan/japan-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1355, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/world/japan/japan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/27/world/japan/japan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/world/japan/japan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Shrine Visit, Leader Asserts Japan’s Track From Pacifism', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Shrine Visit, Leader Asserts Japan’s Track From Pacifism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'ma