'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4a900414-d9e8-5207-97e5-9e31c4166c21', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4a900414-d9e8-5207-97e5-9e31c4166c21'}, {'abstract': 'Critics and writers for The New York Times recommend New Year’s Eve pop music events in the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/27/arts/music/pop-picks-for-new-years-eve-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Critics and writers for The New York Times recommend New Year’s Eve pop music events in the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s over. It’s a wrap. Nothing more to see here. The damage is done. Finis. These are the lies we tell ourselves on New Year’s Eve, a holiday that ostensibly celebrates renewal but really places an arbitrary cap on a year’s worth of love and hurt and strife and joy and seals it, freeing up space to try again, once the ball drops. Going out on New Year’s Eve, then — that’s really for the people who want that jar sealed tight. They want to rage, to get lost in shamanistic fever, to laugh until the body doesn’t know how else to move. Here, writers for The New York Times weigh in on events that, for some, may fill a need for a sense of finality but that are also a reminder that even after the worst, life goes on. JON CARAMANICA', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/arts/27EVE01/27EVE01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/27/arts/27EVE01/27EVE01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/arts/27EVE01/27EVE01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': 