: 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}, {'firstname': 'Megan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Angelo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 5}, {'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 6}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/20f8808c-5b1d-5877-850b-41ad39694d7e', 'word_count': 3496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20f8808c-5b1d-5877-850b-41ad39694d7e'}, {'abstract': 'We are on the cusp of the year, where the past and future intersect.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/27/opinion/life-on-the-edge.html', 'snippet': 'We are on the cusp of the year, where the past and future intersect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN environmental parlance, the ecotone is the zone where two habitats merge, that threshold where water meets the shore, where the forest comes to meadow, or where woodland ends at a cultivated lawn. It is the edge habitat where everything — soil content, vegetation, moisture, humidity, light, pollination — changes. It’s also where species from both sides converge, rendering it a place of complex interaction and diversity. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/opinion/1227OPEDdavis/1227OPEDdavis-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 523, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/27/opinion/1227OPEDdavis/1227OPEDdavis-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 523}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/opinion/1227OPEDdavis/1227OPEDdavis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'le