ETWEEN MAN AND BEAST: An Unlikely Explorer and the African Adventure That Took the Victorian World by Storm,  by Monte Reel. (Anchor, $15.95.) In 1859, Paul Du Chaillu emerged from a four-year hunting expedition in Gabon with a major zoological discovery: 20 preserved skins of a massive ape, which soon took its familiar name — gorilla. Reel’s robust intellectual history recounts Du Chaillu’s exploits and places his “objects of wonder” at the center of the nascent debate over Charles Darwin’s theories about the origin and nature of mankind.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T15:48:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c118e6e-04c1-5113-aa28-3a791d79a515', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c118e6e-04c1-5113-aa28-3a791d79a515'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMERICAN MIRROR: The Life and Art of Norman Rockwell,  by Deborah Solomon. (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $28.) Solomon pays honest respect to Rockwell for his dedication through periods of self-doubt, depression and marital tumult.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T15:48:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/44ad6330-5c27-5d9d-ac81-540395dec781', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44ad6330-5c27-5d9d-ac81-540395dec781'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Burleigh surveys many of the international wars and anticolonial insurgencies during two decades of the Cold War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/books/review/small-wars-faraway-places-by-michael-burleigh.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Burleigh surveys many of the international wars and anticolonial insurgencies during two decades of the Cold War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Small Wars, Faraway Places,” Michael Burleigh recounts the violent end of the British and French empires in Africa and Asia, and their partial replacement by the United States in its often ill-informed and costly efforts to combat Communism during the early stages of the Cold War. Burleigh surveys many of the major international wars and anticolonial insurgencies between 1945 and 1965, but opts to focus most on those involving America, Britain and France, and how they related to the rivalry with the Soviet Union. The result is a well-researched and readable account of two tumultuous decades. Burleigh judges most of these wars, both small and large, to have been futile and destructive. But while he clearly has no interest in defending or rehabilitating such conflicts, he nonetheless offers a fair, thoughtful assessment of the motives and interests behind them. He also takes care to understand and explain the grievances of the insurgents.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29larison-sub/29larison-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 278, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29larison-sub/29larison-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 278}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29larison-sub/29larison-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 474, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29larison-sub/29larison-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 558, 'width': 1206, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29larison-sub/29larison-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29larison-sub/29larison-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29larison-sub/29larison-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bullet Diplomacy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bullet Diplomacy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burleigh, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T15:49:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Larison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Larison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6669f0e-cb0f-5dec-8aa3-d563a74d7592', 'word_count': 1153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6669f0e-cb0f-5dec-8aa3-d563a74d7592'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Jack London, whose fictional characters lived in a determined universe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/books/review/jack-london-an-american-life-by-earle-labor.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Jack London, whose fictional characters lived in a determined universe.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The superficial reader will get the love story & the adventure,” Jack London wrote, in 1903, of the story that would become “The Sea-Wolf,” “while the deeper reader will get all of this, plus the bigger thing lying underneath.” These are the typically blunt words of a writer who seemed to think of his work in terms of purchase value. Here, he might have been describing his own life: much adventure, a sort of love story, a weird bang of a finish. In a new biography by the London scholar Earle Labor, the “bigger thing” has a harder time coming out: Perhaps it doesn’t exist.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29GIARDINA/29GIARDINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29GIARDINA/29GIARDINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 670}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29GIARDINA/29GIARDINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 917, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29GIARDINA/29GIARDINA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1834, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29GIARDINA/29GIARDINA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29GIARDINA/29GIARDINA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29GIARDINA/29GIARDINA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Man Against Nature', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Man Against Nature', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Labor, Earle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'London, Jack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T15:49:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Giardina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giardina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/82281d5b-ad06-5f85-ab98-10630d72f703', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82281d5b-ad06-5f85-ab98-10630d72f703'}, {'abstract': 'Frederick Busch’s plain-spoken stories culminate in often unsettling realizations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/books/review/the-stories-of-frederick-busch.html', 'snippet': 'Frederick Busch’s plain-spoken stories culminate in often unsettling realizations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine the writer as a wager of war. Some stun with shock-and-awe prose, surprise plot twists, barrages of literary ordnance. Others deploy steady blasts of sentiment, turning heartstrings into so many tripwires. But some conduct a subtler campaign, built on stealth maneuvers: escalating schemes, deft and imperceptible, that quietly, thrillingly, devastate. \xadFrederick Busch, the author of more than two dozen books, who died in 2006 at the age of 64, was a master of this approach. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29ARNOLDRATLIFF/29ARNOLDRATLIFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29ARNOLDRATLIFF/29ARNOLDRATLIFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 792}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29ARNOLDRATLIFF/29ARNOLDRATLIFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29ARNOLDRATLIFF/29ARNOLDRATLIFF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1542, 'width': 1168, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29ARNOLDRATLIFF/29ARNOLDRATLIFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29ARNOLDRATLIFF/29ARNOLDRATLIFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29ARNOLDRATLIFF/29ARNOLDRATLIFF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stealth Maneuvers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stealth Maneuvers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Busch, Frederick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strout, Elizabeth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T15:50:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Arnold-Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arnold-Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6c9f372-ff74-57d7-8cd0-2e8eb5bc6873', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6c9f372-ff74-57d7-8cd0-2e8eb5bc6873'}, {'abstract': 'Sam Thompson’s first novel puts urban life under a microscope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/books/review/sam-thompsons-communion-town.html', 'snippet': 'Sam Thompson’s first novel puts urban life under a microscope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is tempting to call “Communion Town” a novel about a city. But neither “novel” nor “city” quite captures the eerie multi\xaddimensionality of Sam Thompson’s dazzling debut; everything about “Communion Town” — from its genre to its subject to its shifting perspectives — proves to be more complicated than it seems. At the end of the book, the city remains as hazy to readers as shadows on the walls of a cave. What “Communion Town” renders with stunning and often heartbreaking lucidity is the complexity of consciousness itself. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Dubois/1229-bks-Dubois-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Dubois/1229-bks-Dubois-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Dubois/1229-bks-Dubois-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Dubois/1229-bks-Dubois-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 1599, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Dubois/1229-bks-Dubois-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Dubois/1229-bks-Dubois-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Dubois/1229-bks-Dubois-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Close Quarters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Close Quarters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'duBois, Jennifer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Sam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T15:50:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Dubois', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dubois', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9ec8d58-0d11-56b8-aa4e-5dc9db62554e', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9ec8d58-0d11-56b8-aa4e-5dc9db62554e'}, {'abstract': 'An Iranian-American recounts his yearlong sojourn in Tehran.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/books/review/the-ministry-of-guidance-invites-you-to-not-stay-by-hooman-majd.html', 'snippet': 'An Iranian-American recounts his yearlong sojourn in Tehran.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To write about one’s country while living in another is to invite questions about loyalty. Why are you writing this? And for whom? The questions can take an ominous tone: What is your agenda? The journalist Hooman Majd faced such suspicions on one of his trips to Tehran, when an employee from the Ministry of Culture and Islamic Guidance told him bluntly: “Just because you have an Iranian passport doesn’t mean you can come here and write whatever you want when you leave.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29LALAMI/29LALAMI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29LALAMI/29LALAMI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 906}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29LALAMI/29LALAMI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 678, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29LALAMI/29LALAMI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1356, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29LALAMI/29LALAMI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29LALAMI/29LALAMI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/29LALAMI/29LALAMI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Guests of the Ayatollah', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guests of the Ayatollah', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lalami, Laila', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Majd, Hooman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T15:51:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laila Lalami', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lalami', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d90f14e-f133-5957-b48f-1d0aa01e496b', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d90f14e-f133-5957-b48f-1d0aa01e496b'}, {'abstract': 'The Yale psychologist Paul Bloom mines child-development research for insights into morality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/books/review/just-babies-the-origins-of-good-and-evil-by-paul-bloom.html', 'snippet': 'The Yale psychologist Paul Bloom mines child-development research for insights into morality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is morality innate? In his new book, “Just Babies,” the psychologist Paul Bloom draws from his research at the Yale Infant Cognition Center to argue that “certain moral foundations are not acquired through learning. . . . They are instead the products of biological evolution.” Infants may be notoriously difficult to study (rats and pigeons “can at least run mazes or peck at levers”), but according to Bloom, they are, in fact, “moral creatures.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Baron-Cohen/1229-bks-Baron-Cohen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Baron-Cohen/1229-bks-Baron-Cohen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 661}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/books/review/1229-bks-Baron-Cohen/1229-bks-Baron-Cohen-jumbo.jpg', 'height'