Standard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/28/business/Shortcuts1/Shortcuts1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Inventions and Cellphone Contracts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Revisiting Inventions And Cellphone Contracts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tugend, Alina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drivers Licenses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'T-Mobile US Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AT&T Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Quirky Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T17:14:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alina Tugend', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tugend', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c85e255-d97d-5bb7-847f-4636b2b9f125', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c85e255-d97d-5bb7-847f-4636b2b9f125'}, {'abstract': 'Booms are nice, slumps are bad. Why?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/27/on-the-asymmetry-of-booms-and-slumps-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'Booms are nice, slumps are bad. Why?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Something I’m thinking about: Recently Antonio Fatas listed four ingredients that he considered missing in macroeconomic models, with the first element being the asymmetry of the business cycle. Basically, the standard models say that output fluctuates around a normal level, with the ups and downs equal in size, and that all policy can do is reduce the variance. Fatas believes, however, that the business cycle is better seen as occasional drops below potential output, then returns to trend — a view that makes good policy much more important. I agree. But why?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Asymmetry of Booms and Slumps (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-27T17:18:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50463388-19d9-5279-bd51-aea086b427e6', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50463388-19d9-5279-bd51-aea086b427e6'}, {'abstract': 'When the going gets steep, the smart use winch cats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/12/27/automobiles/28SNOWCAT.html', 'snippet': 'When the going gets steep, the smart use winch cats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the going gets steep, the smart use winch cats.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/27/automobiles/28SNOWCAT-slide-NKZ7/28SNOWCAT-slide-NKZ7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/27/automobiles/28SNOWCAT-slide-NKZ7/28SNOWCAT-slide-NKZ7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'im