material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d57ef6cc-a7ce-5aca-bbae-0516a2ea12c0', 'word_count': 1012, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d57ef6cc-a7ce-5aca-bbae-0516a2ea12c0'}, {'abstract': 'Martha Rose Shulman, the Recipes for Health columnist, cooks with oranges, grapefruit and more.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/27/adding-citrus-to-salads-and-desserts/', 'snippet': 'Martha Rose Shulman, the Recipes for Health columnist, cooks with oranges, grapefruit and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I always stuff an orange or a tangerine into the toe of my son’s Christmas stocking. He is more interested in the chocolate in his stocking and I usually end up eating his orange, but I’ll never forego this European tradition that dates from a time when citrus was a rare treat. We in the United States take our oranges and grapefruit for granted year round, but citrus really does have a season — winter — and it is welcome at this time of year, when we need all the vitamin C we can get. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/24/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/24/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'captio