a local official who has provided residents with letters describing their area’s drug violence to help them seek asylum in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/world/americas/path-to-asylum-for-mexicans-bearing-letter.html', 'snippet': 'Mexican officials have become frustrated by a local official who has provided residents with letters describing their area’s drug violence to help them seek asylum in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LA RUANA, Mexico — Jittery families cram into his tiny office here, daily. Hundreds more have appeared at the San Diego border 1,500 miles away, clutching an official-looking letter bearing his name, gambling that its description of the violence in this blistering stretch of central Mexico will help them gain asylum in the United States.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/world/mexico/mexico-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/29/world/mexico/mexico-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/world/mexico/mexico-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/world/mexico/mexico-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1347, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/world/mexico/mexico-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/29/world/mexico/mexico-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/29/world/mexico/mexico-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Civil Servant in Mexico Tests U.S. on Asylum', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Civil Servant In Mexico Tests U.S. on Asylum', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cave, Damien', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Cartels', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Contreras Orozco, C Ramon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:19:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damien Cave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1346f73a-b09b-55dd-9e02-0e353d784c10', 'word_count': 1473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1346f73a-b09b-55dd-9e02-0e353d784c10'}, {'abstract': 'Federal unemployment benefits expire while Congress enjoys a holiday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/opinion/sunday/no-cheer-for-the-jobless.html', 'snippet': 'Federal unemployment benefits expire while Congress enjoys a holiday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The House Republicans who refused to renew expiring federal jobless benefits before leaving Washington for an extended Christmas break have shown no inclination to revisit the issue when they return to work in January. This is unconscionable. In each of the previous seven major recessions, dating back to 1958, Congress has never let federal benefits expire when the need has been as great as it is today. And for good reason. Prematurely ending benefits inflicts needless harm on families — the average benefit is about $270 a week — as well as avoidable damage on the economy by denting essential spending. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Cheer for the Jobless', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Cheer for the Jobless', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment Insurance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/36948036-3417-5188-a4d0-b402f7c9bcdc', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36948036-3417-5188-a4d0-b402f7c9bcdc'}, {'abstract': 'Despite policy mistakes, he will leave a lasting and largely beneficial imprint on New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/opinion/sunday/12-years-of-mayor-bloomberg.html', 'snippet': 'Despite policy mistakes, he will leave a lasting and largely beneficial imprint on New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When he walked into the mayor’s office 12 years ago, businessman and billionaire Michael Bloomberg took charge of a damaged city. The World Trade Center was a windblown construction site, barely cleared after the attacks on Sept. 11. Tourists were afraid to come to the city; residents were afraid to stay. The budget was a disaster, $3 billion to $5 billion in the red. In a modest speech at an intentionally modest inauguration, Mr. Bloomberg nevertheless pledged to rebuild and renew New York and to keep it “the capital of the free world.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '12 Years of Mayor Bloomberg', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '12 Years of Mayor Bloomberg', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mayors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/69b9709e-83f2-5776-b3f4-03b1a7aface2', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69b9709e-83f2-5776-b3f4-03b1a7aface2'}, {'abstract': 'The most annoying locutions of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/opinion/egan-words-for-the-dumpster.html', 'snippet': 'The most annoying locutions of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WITH the last tick of 2013, let’s throw out the most annoying, overused and abused words of the year. A few of these terms, “twerking” or “stay classy,” die a natural death when someone like John McCain starts using them — the aural equivalent of a comb-over. Others need a push. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/opinion/timothy-egan/timothy-egan-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/11/opinion/timothy-egan/timothy-egan-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/opinion/timothy-egan/timothy-egan-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/opinion/timothy-egan/timothy-egan-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/opinion/timothy-egan/timothy-egan-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/11/opinion/timothy-egan/timothy-egan-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/opinion/timothy-egan/timothy-egan-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Words for the Dumpster', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Words for the Dumpster', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Egan, Timothy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e296734c-dcc2-59ff-ab5a-119e07c6d344', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e296734c-dcc2-59ff-ab5a-119e07c6d344'}, {'abstract': 'In Africa, encounters with a God different from that of United States evangelicals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/opinion/sunday/when-demons-are-real.html', 'snippet': 'In Africa, encounters with a God different from that of United States evangelicals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOME months ago, on a warm Friday evening in Accra, Ghana, I found myself in an all-night prayer session with a charismatic evangelical church. All-night prayer has become wildly popular in the city, somewhat to the distress of those who object to the late-night noise, which rivals that of an American frat party. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/05/opinion/tanya-lurmann/tanya-lurmann-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Demons Are Real', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Demons Are Real', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luhrmann, T M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evangelical Movement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ghana', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:30:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By T. M. Luhrmann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'T.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bcc591f-7e4e-58e8-8ecd-ef89dea2f914', 'word_count': 925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bcc591f-7e4e-58e8-8ecd-ef89dea2f914'}, {'abstract': 'Here is a look at my three biggest mistakes of 2013.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/opinion/sunday/douthat-confessions-of-a-columnist.html', 'snippet': 'Here is a look at my three biggest mistakes of 2013.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN ancient Sanskrit, the word “pundit” meant “wise man” or “religious sage.” In modern English, it means “often wrong, rarely accountable.” There are ways that those of us who scribble about politics can avoid living down to that reputation — by keeping our predictions vague (it worked for Nostradamus), by sticking to sure things (I told you Herman Cain wouldn’t be elected president), or by deploying weasel words like “it’s possible that ...” at every opportunity. But time, chance and fallibility eventually make false prophets of us all.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Confessions of a Columnist', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Confessions Of a Columnist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douthat, Ross G', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:31:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/dda9f5f6-1ff6-56d5-a4be-73d13ffb6c5a', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dda9f5f6-1ff6-56d5-a4be-73d13ffb6c5a'}, {'abstract': 'People who brandish guns, threaten others and even admit to mental illness are able to get around the police.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/opinion/sunday/when-the-mentally-ill-own-guns.html', 'snippet': 'People who brandish guns, threaten others and even admit to mental illness are able to get around the police.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawmakers who refuse to support effective gun safety measures often prefer to talk about better screening of the mentally ill to identify deranged would-be perpetrators before they can carry out mass shootings. This is, of course, a political dodge. Even in the handful of states where law enforcement agencies are trying to confiscate the guns of unstable individuals, state and federal laws too often enable the mentally ill to reclaim their guns as a right under the Second Amendment. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When the Mentally Ill Own Guns', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When the Mentally Ill Own Guns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:32:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a694dec-feec-5a11-9739-24ed23d01493', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a694dec-feec-5a11-9739-24ed23d01493'}, {'abstract': 'There’s an agenda there, and it’s a bad one.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/28/bitcoin-is-evil/', 'snippet': 'There’s an agenda there, and it’s a bad one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s always important, and always hard, to distinguish positive economics — how things work — from normative economics — how things should be. Indeed, on many of the macro issues I’ve written about it has been obvious that large numbers of economists can’t bring themselves to make that distinction; they dislike activist government on political grounds, and this leads them to make really bad arguments about why fiscal stimulus can’t work and monetary stimulus will be disastrous. I don’t, by the way, think that this effect is symmetric: although people like Robert Lucas were quick to accuse people like Christy Romer of fabricating macro arguments to support a big-government agenda, this didn’t actually happen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bitcoin Is Evil', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-28T19:35:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aed7aac5-43ad-5b06-995d-f0bc28327e18', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aed7aac5-43ad-5b06-995d-f0bc28327e18'}, {'abstract': 'They didn’t say what they said, they say.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/28/the-year-of-the-weasel/', 'snippet': 'They didn’t say what they said, they say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just a brief thought about what didn’t happen in 2013, and what did.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year of the Weasel', 'ki