Volgograd (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-29T16:01:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'Reuters', 'person': [], 'organization': 'Reuters'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/45c778a7-213d-52ab-bbef-1278f6477039', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/45c778a7-213d-52ab-bbef-1278f6477039'}, {'abstract': 'Many noted indie video games were released in 2013, but major studios grabbed the headlines.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/game-theory-the-year-of-the-blockbuster/', 'snippet': 'Many noted indie video games were released in 2013, but major studios grabbed the headlines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I look back at a year of playing video games in 2013, many of my favorite moments come from the putatively soulless games made by hundreds of people on behalf of multinational corporations. Among them are Ellie’s discovery of the giraffes in Utah in The Last of Us, along with the joy of getting to play as Ellie in the sequence called “Winter,” and the bleakness of the game’s final seconds, which sit alongside the end of the movie “Blue Jasmine” as the most unforgiving closing scenes in all of entertainment this year. Also memorable is Trevor’s May-December romance with Patricia Madrazo in Grand Theft Auto V, revealing the tender side of a psychopath, capped by the long, bittersweet drive back to Martin Madrazo’s house while listening to Chicago’s “If You Leave Me Now.” And in BioShock Infinite what stuck with me was everything from the game’s audacious first hour or so, the racist Boxer Rebellion and Battle of Wounded Knee exhibitions from the Hall of Heroes, and listening to Elizabeth sing “Will the Circle Be Unbroken.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Game Theory: The Year of the Blockbuster', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldberg, Harold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Totilo, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-29T16:54:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Suellentrop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suellentrop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f972bf8-e4bc-5601-bb5f-b656bf5ba48d', 'word_count': 423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f972bf8-e4bc-5601-bb5f-b656bf5ba48d'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/29/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '1:15 P.M. (TMC) DICK TRACY (1990) Warren Beatty, right, directed and stars in this brightly colored adaptation of the comic strip created by Chester Gould. Madonna is Breathless Mahoney, the resident femme fatale, and Al Pacino plays Big Boy Caprice, a ruthless mobster with a proclivity for walnuts. “\xa0‘Dick Tracy’ has just about everything required of an extravaganza: a smashing cast, some great Stephen Sondheim songs, all of the technical wizardry that money can buy (plus the knowledge of how and when to use it), and a screenplay (credited to Jim Cash and Jack Epps Jr.) that observes the fine line separating true comedy from lesser camp,” Vincent Canby wrote in The New York Times. The film won three Oscars, including those for best makeup and best original song for Mr. Sondheim’s “Sooner or Later (I Always Get My Man).”', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-29T17:13:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c954819-9a31-56ab-b840-860ea8dd459a', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c954819-9a31-56ab-b840-860ea8dd459a'}, {'abstract': 'The march included protesters with a diverse list of grievances: Buddhist monks, garment workers, farmers and supporters of the main opposition party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/world/asia/antigovernment-march-draws-broad-coalition-of-protesters-in-cambodia.html', 'snippet': 'The march included protesters with a diverse list of grievances: Buddhist monks, garment workers, farmers and supporters of the main opposition party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHNOM PENH, Cambodia — Tens of thousands of antigovernment demonstrators marched through Phnom Penh on Sunday in one of the biggest acts of defiance against the nearly three decades of rule by Cambodia’s authoritarian prime minister, Hun Sen.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/world/asia/30cambodia-img/30cambodia-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/30/world/asia/30cambodia-img/30cambodia-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/world/asia/30cambodia-img/30cambodia-i