mages/2013/12/31/us/31HEALTH/31HEALTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/31/us/31HEALTH/31HEALTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/us/31HEALTH/31HEALTH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Second Official to Leave After Health Site Trouble', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Second Official to Leave After Health Site Trouble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pear, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HealthCare.gov', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snyder, A Michelle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-30T16:26:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40671db3-17d0-53fd-9abb-6d746bec0596', 'word_count': 695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40671db3-17d0-53fd-9abb-6d746bec0596'}, {'abstract': 'For years before John Wilkes Booth assassinated Abraham Lincoln, their families intersected in eerily coincidental ways.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/the-lincolns-and-the-booths/', 'snippet': 'For years before John Wilkes Booth assassinated Abraham Lincoln, their families intersected in eerily coincidental ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over a year before firing the shot that killed Abraham Lincoln, the assassin John Wilkes Booth began to stalk his victim by worming his way into Lincoln’s social circles. But even as the young actor pressed in, Lincoln’s friends and acquaintances began to cross his path through a series of bizarre, random personal encounters. Fate, it seemed, was stalking the stalker. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lincolns and the Booths', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inaugurations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Booth, Edwin Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Booth, John Wilkes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-30T16:29:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Morrow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morrow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a93829b5-7173-5380-8405-0e843a52f9ad', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a93829b5-7173-5380-8405-0e843a52f9ad'}, {'abstract': 'At a luncheon for “The Wolf of Wall Street,” Matthew McConaughey discusses his performance in the film.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/video-matthew-mcconaughey-on-the-wolf-of-wall-street/', 'snippet': 'At a luncheon for “The Wolf of Wall Street,” Matthew McConaughey discusses his performance in the film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Matthew McConaughey has had a busy awards season. Although his leading role in the drama “Dallas Buyer’s Club” is garnering most of the Oscar attention, the small part he has in Martin Scorsese’s “Wolf of Wall Street” also has critics talking (and humming). As a stock trader who teaches a few tricks of the trade to his new employee Jordan Belfort, Mr. McConaughey delivers his dialogue with a sharp tongue and a greedy spirit. In this video, from a recent luncheon for the film, he spoke about that scene and, in particular, the chest-thumping song that he hums in it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Matthew McConaughey on ‘The Wolf of Wall Street’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConaughey, Matthew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-30T16:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edd49217-8da7-53a1-8ca6-b19ec45dbaf3', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edd49217-8da7-53a1-8ca6-b19ec45dbaf3'}, {'abstract': 'A designer for the studio Naughty Dog offers a perspective on women as video game characters and as part of the industry.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/game-theory-the-last-of-us-revisited/', 'snippet': 'A designer for the studio Naughty Dog offers a perspective on women as video game characters and as part of the industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chris Suellentrop reviewed The Last of Us, a post-apocalyptic video game drama from the studio Naughty Dog, for The New York Times in June. After that, a member of the team that made the game wrote a rebuttal letter. Below is a condensed and edited version of that essay. A little working knowledge of the game — including familiarity with characters like Ellie, David and Joel — is helpful for following along.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Game Theory: The Last of Us, Revisited', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Naughty Dog Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-30T16:34:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandria Neonakis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Neonakis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c273399-a223-507e-8f6e-1ef6df424677', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c273399-a223-507e-8f6e-1ef6df424677'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from Volgograd, where an explosion on a trolley bus killed at least 15 people on Monday. The attack came a day after a suicide bombing at the city’s main railroad station.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/europe/100000002625673/second-deadly-blast-hits-russia.html', 'snippet': 'Scenes from Volgograd, where an explosion on a trolley bus killed at least 15 people on Monday. The attack came a day after a suicide bombing at the city’s main railroad station.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scenes from Volgograd, where an explosion on a trolley bus killed at least 15 people on Monday. The attack cam