cous with the chili paste better in this pretty frittata than I did on its own. You won’t use up all of the zhoug in the frittata but you will be glad to have the relish on hand to use as a condiment. Note that I do not cook my Israeli couscous in boiling water; I find that it becomes too mushy that way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/health/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/03/health/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/health/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/health/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/health/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/03/health/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/health/03recipehealth/03recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Israeli Couscous and Spicy Herb Paste Frittata', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salad Dressings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-30T16:50:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/0badeeac-dbfd-5c19-9a7f-c5952a072b69', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0badeeac-dbfd-5c19-9a7f-c5952a072b69'}, {'abstract': 'Can you help Indianapolis Colts quarterback Andrew Luck solve this monster problem?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/luck-2/', 'snippet': 'Can you help Indianapolis Colts quarterback Andrew Luck solve this monster problem?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our puzzle this week was suggested by Andrew Luck, the record-setting quarterback of the Indianapolis Colts. Mr. Luck was the N.F.L.’s number one draft pick in 2012 and led a remarkable turnaround of his team during his first year, taking it from 2-14 in 2011 to 11-5 in 2012. This year the Colts continue to thrive under Luck’s leadership, finishing the regular season atop their division with an 11-5 record.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/03/crosswords/Andrew-Luck/Andrew-Luck-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/03/crosswords/Andrew-Luck/Andrew-Luck-articleLarge.jpg