January emphatically rejected the candidacies of players from the steroids era like Barry Bonds, Roger Clemens and Sammy Sosa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was inevitable that the steroids era would intrude on the business of the Baseball Hall of Fame. Mark McGwire had been rejected six times by the Baseball Writers’ Association of America when Barry Bonds, Roger Clemens and Sammy Sosa missed out for the first time last January. In his seventh rejection, McGwire received 16.9 percent of the vote, less than half of Bonds’s 36.2 percent and far short of the 75 percent required for election.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/sports/yearendhall/yearendhall-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/31/sports/yearendhall/yearendhall-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/sports/yearendhall/yearendhall-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/sports/yearendhall/yearendhall-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/sports/yearendhall/yearendhall-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/31/sports/yearendhall/yearendhall-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/sports/yearendhall/yearendhall-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Quiet Summer in Cooperstown', 'kicker': '2013: A Look Back, and Beyond', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Quiet Summer in Cooperstown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baseball Hall of Fame', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baseball Writers Assn of America', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bonds, Barry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGwire, Mark', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sosa, Sammy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clemens, Roger', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-30T18:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ffeffcb-3e6a-5c2a-aa0c-2406653afbf1', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ffeffcb-3e6a-5c2a-aa0c-2406653afbf1'}, {'abstract': 'So you can be too cynical.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/bankers-beaten-back-a-bit/', 'snippet': 'So you can be too cynical.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mike Konczal has some surprising news: Financial reform is getting somewhere. We entered 2013 amid universal cynicism; the bankers had bought Washington again, and any reforms would be purely cosmetic. We leave it with significant progress. You can still argue that reform falls far short of what should have happened, and won’t do much to prevent future crises. But relative to expectations, this is a happy story.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bankers Beaten Back A Bit', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-30T18:58:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cc25482-8c5e-561d-845e-9df531e2ebe2', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cc25482-8c5e-561d-845e-9df531e2ebe2'}, {'abstract': 'The crew on Pharrell Williams’s 24-hour “Happy” video were overwhelmed when the production moved into Magic Johnson’s trophy room.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/30/a-happy-moment-with-magic-johnson/', 'snippet': 'The crew on Pharrell Williams’s 24-hour “Happy” video were overwhelmed when the production moved into Magic Johnson’s trophy room.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Need a post-holiday (or pre-holiday) pick-me-up? Click over to “24 Hours of Happy,” the addictive video for the Pharrell Williams song “Happy,” featured in “Despicable Me 2.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/arts/magichappy/magichappy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 228, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/30/arts/magichappy/magichappy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 228}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 