ld save lives? My daughter says we’re selfish. My son says we do enough.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/31/does-philanthropic-peer-pressure-or-morality-matter-more/', 'snippet': 'Why do we continue to live relatively decadently when the money we spend on organic dishwasher detergent could save lives? My daughter says we’re selfish. My son says we do enough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do you know the philosopher Peter Singer’s thought exercise about charitable giving? It’s this: Imagine you’re on your way to work, but you see a child who has fallen into a pond and appears to be drowning. The idea of ruining your shoes or suit — or even, say, your brand-new Christmas iPhone — would not keep you from rescuing that child. You would, in fact, do it again the next day. So, why don’t we? What is keeping us from giving more to ill and malnourished children around the world, whose lives could be saved by the cost of a pair of shoes?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/style/motherlode-mudroom2/motherlode-mudroom2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/30/style/motherlode-mudroom2/motherlode-mudroom2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/style/motherlode-mudroom2/motherlode-mudroom2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/style/motherlode-mudroom2/motherlode-mudroom2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1632, 'width': 1224, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/style/motherlode-mudroom2/motherlode-mudroom2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/30/style/motherlode-mudroom2/motherlode-mudroom2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/style/motherlode-mudroom2/motherlode-mudroom2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Philanthropic Peer Pressure or Morality Matter More?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singer, Peter (1946- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-12-31T17:10:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/027ed278-5016-55d2-98f7-b756c93a1a24', 'word_count': 913, 'uri': 'nyt://article/027ed278-5016-55d2-98f7-b756c93a1a24'}, {'abstract': 'A denarius for your thoughts.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/12/31/266-and-all-that/', 'snippet': 'A denarius for your thoughts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ah, some of the comments on my post about cynical lack of realism happen to illustrate a favorite observation of mine: the extent to which people who demand that we learn the lessons of history tend to rely on historical episodes in which we have very little idea of what really happened. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '266 And All That', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-12-31T17:20:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9df03c1c-96cf-52f8-ba84-e57f0bf24449', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9df03c1c-96cf-52f8-ba84-e57f0bf24449'}, {'abstract': 'Times reporters and columnists look back at the Bloomberg legacy in some of the key areas that most affect New Yorkers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/12/31/nyregion/the-bloomberg-legacy.html', 'snippet': 'Times reporters and columnists look back at the Bloomberg legacy in some of the key areas that most affect New Yorkers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Times reporters and columnists look back at the Bloomberg legacy in some of the key areas that most affect New Yorkers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/nyregion/31BLOOMBERG-VIDEOS/31BLOOMBERG-VIDEOS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/31/nyregion/31BLOOMBERG-VIDEOS/31BLOOMBERG-VIDEOS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/31/n